// tcp.mtp — the MTP program this target runs.
//
// It knows nothing about the target's runtime, its instructions'
// implementations, or DPDK. All protocol logic lives here.
//
// THE AUTHORITY. src/program/*.c is what the compiler emits from this file.
// Every processor there cites its section as `mtp/tcp.mtp §name`, and
// tools/check_wiring.sh fails the build if a citation points at a section that
// is not here — because the prototype's own tcp.mtp was byte-identical across
// five branches while the C it supposedly described was rewritten twice, and a
// program file nobody checks is worse than none.
//
// SCOPE. docs/PLAN.md §6.1 M1c and everything since: passive AND active open,
// bulk send under cwnd and rwnd, acknowledgement generation to the donor's
// rule, out-of-order reassembly, fast retransmit, PAWS and sequence
// validation, the closed-window probe, reset handling in both directions, FIN
// teardown through TIME_WAIT, and the retransmission timeout. What is
// deliberately absent is declared in §absence_register, not left to be
// inferred.
//
// PARITY. Every constant is the DONOR'S RUNNING CONFIGURATION, not a standard
// and not memory (rule 1). The ones that look like defects are reproduced on
// purpose and say so. docs/DESIGN.md §7.2 is the freeze; the SYN-ACK it
// produces was measured field-for-field against the donor's on 2026-08-12
// (docs/RESULTS.md) and matched.
//
// DIALECT. Written in the surface language of MTP-kernel-test's frozen v4
// (docs/MTP_LANG.md) plus the extensions this target needs it to have. Every
// extension is listed in docs/LANGUAGE-NEEDS.md under "Surface forms this
// program uses"; the lead reconciles the two dialects. Nothing here is
// improvised in silence. In outline, this program uses:
//
//   const declarations and hex literals; a conditional expression; explicit
//   width casts (`int32(x)`) for sequence arithmetic, which is what makes
//   "is this acknowledgement old?" sayable at all; `opt_t`/`opt_union`/
//   `opt_list` with a parser-side `has_<option>` accessor; a header-length
//   field the compiler fills (`hdrlen4_t`), in the sense `checksum16_t` is
//   already a field the target fills; a timer bound to the event it raises;
//   a `coalesce` declaration (§coalesce);
//   ordinary functions beside processors; and instructions CALLED rather than
//   collected into a `list<instr_t>`, because this target executes them where
//   they are written -- which is why `pkt_gen`'s and `rx_flush_and_notify`'s
//   return values are protocol state here.

// §types
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `addr_t` and `rx_addr_t` ARE DIFFERENT TYPES, and the frozen language has one.
// MTP_LANG 2 says addr_t is "split by direction" at the runtime; in this target
// they are two concrete types with different fields, and one app event carries
// each -- `send` a transmit reference, `recv` a receive one -- so direction
// cannot be recovered from the event's base. The program says which.
//
// ONE TYPE THE FROZEN LANGUAGE DOES NOT HAVE, and it is here because the
// application interface forces it. A flow is named by naming its CONTEXT, or
// by its flow id across the application interface; the program never holds a
// target handle.
// jobs —
//
//   the handle instructions take          (`pkt_gen`, `notify`, `retry`)
//   the accept backlog, which holds completed connections
//   the value an op-parser hands back to a synchronous socket call
//
// The third is CR-2 (docs/CONTRACT-REQUESTS.md): `accept()` and `listen()` must
// answer with a flow, and a queued, kind-coalesced notification cannot answer a
// synchronous call. The lead's ruling was "the processor writes op->flow, no
// result type, no new instruction", so the OUT field is declared on the event
// and the generated dispatch copies it back.
//
// A context reference is NOT such a type. A connection needs to reach the
// listener that accepted it; it does so by keeping the listener's KEY and
// looking it up, because contexts in MTP are keyed rather than pointed at.

// §constants
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
const uint8  FLAG_FIN = 0x01;
const uint8  FLAG_SYN = 0x02;
const uint8  FLAG_RST = 0x04;
const uint8  FLAG_PSH = 0x08;
const uint8  FLAG_ACK = 0x10;

const uint8  ST_CLOSED      = 0;
const uint8  ST_LISTEN      = 1;
const uint8  ST_SYN_RCVD    = 2;
const uint8  ST_ESTABLISHED = 3;
const uint8  ST_CLOSE_WAIT  = 4;
const uint8  ST_LAST_ACK    = 5;
// The active-close path. Absent until 2026-08-13, which is why this program
// could not initiate a close at all against a peer that never closes first.
const uint8  ST_FIN_WAIT_1  = 6;
const uint8  ST_FIN_WAIT_2  = 7;
const uint8  ST_CLOSING     = 8;   // simultaneous close
const uint8  ST_TIME_WAIT   = 9;
// The active-OPEN path, absent until 2026-08-25 for the same reason: nothing
// we ran ever opened a connection, so the state had no way to be entered.
const uint8  ST_SYN_SENT    = 10;

const uint16 PARITY_MSS_ADVERTISED = 1460;   // what the option carries
const uint16 PARITY_MSS_PAYLOAD    = 1448;   // mss - CalculateOptionLength(ACK), which
                                      // the donor computes inline at
                                      // tcp_out.c:566. There is no `eff_mss` in
                                      // the donor to grep for.
const uint8  PARITY_WSCALE         = 7;      // sent always
const uint32 PARITY_RCVBUF_SIZE         = 262144; // the donor's running rcvbuf
const uint32 PARITY_INITIAL_WINDOW = 14600;  // tcp_stream.c:325

const uint32 PARITY_ISN            = 0;      // ALWAYS. Reproduce, do not correct.
const uint32 PARITY_INIT_CWND      = 2920;   // 2 * MSS
const uint32 PARITY_SSTHRESH_ACTIVE = 14600; // MSS * 10

// D-01. mTCP never assigns ssthresh on the passive-open path, so the server —
// the machine under test — runs with ssthresh 0, the slow-start test is false
// from the first acknowledgement, and it does congestion avoidance from
// cwnd = 2*MSS. Reproduced knowingly; PARITY_OPENING_TRAJECTORY gates it.
//
// The flag covers snd_wl1 and last_ack_seq too, and the reason is subtle: only
// ssthresh has a live consequence TODAY, because snd_wl1's divergence is masked
// by the PARITY_ISN freeze above. Lift the PARITY_ISN and it activates silently, with nothing
// in a diff pointing at the cause. The two freezes are coupled.
const bool   PARITY_OPENING_TRAJECTORY = true;
const uint32 PARITY_SSTHRESH_PASSIVE = 0;

const uint32 PARITY_DUP_ACK_THRESH = 3;      // tcp_in.c:417 — the donor acts on the
                                      // third duplicate and only the third
const uint32 PARITY_MAX_RTX        = 16;     // TCP_MAX_RTX, enforced
const uint32 PARITY_MAX_SYN_RETRY  = 7;      // SYN_SENT only; timer.c:267-269
const uint32 PARITY_INITIAL_RTO_MS = 500;    // until the first round-trip sample only
const uint32 PARITY_TIMEWAIT_MS    = 0;      // the donor's running configuration. Zero
                                      // is a REAL VALUE, not "unset" — D-24:
                                      // the state must be entered and left even
                                      // at zero, because it is the place the
                                      // final acknowledgement is owed from.
const uint32 PARITY_PROBE_MS       = 500;    // D-25 piece 2: the donor probes a closed
                                      // window 500 ms after the last
                                      // acknowledgement it SENT, with no backoff
const uint32 PARITY_IDLE_MS        = 30000;  // conf/aqua/upcheck.conf `tcp_timeout = 30`
const uint32 PROG_MAX_BACKLOG    = 128;    // the compile-time bound on one listener's
                                      // accept queue; `pending_cap` below it is
                                      // the runtime bound the application asked
                                      // for, and they are different questions

// PSH is never set. Tested at tcp_out.c:172,265 and passed by no caller. Listed
// because this program builds the header, so a flag bit can diverge with no
// protocol logic wrong anywhere.
const bool   PARITY_SET_PSH = false;

// THE PROGRAM'S TRANSMIT SCHEDULING POLICY (D-17). The target provides
// priority classes and drains the highest first; it attaches no meaning to
// them. What each class MEANS is stated HERE and nowhere else — that is what
// keeps the protocol out of the target while still producing the donor's
// observable ordering: control ahead of pure acknowledgements ahead of data.
//
// THE FIN IS PRIO_DATA, NOT PRIO_CONTROL. A FIN occupies a sequence position
// after the data it terminates, and class 2 drains before class 0, so
// classifying it as control sends it AHEAD of the payload it follows: the peer
// sees a FIN at a sequence number it has not reached and the transfer never
// completes. Measured, not reasoned. The rule this is an instance of is that
// ANYTHING WHICH CONSUMES SEQUENCE SPACE MUST BE ORDERED WITH THE DATA.
const uint8  PRIO_CONTROL = 2;        // SYN: holds no data position
const uint8  PRIO_ACK     = 1;        // pure acknowledgements and window probes
const uint8  PRIO_DATA    = 0;        // payload — and the FIN, per above

// The donor's EnqueueACK options (tcp_out.c:1082), named after it because the
// whole rule is "do what the donor does" and a different name here would be a
// place for the two to drift apart.
const uint8  ACK_NONE      = 0;       // this segment owes nothing, which is NOT
                                      // the same as owing an aggregated one
const uint8  ACK_AGGREGATE = 1;       // raises the count to 1 if it is 0
const uint8  ACK_APIECE    = 2;       // INCREMENTS the count: one of its own

// §transport
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THE INFRASTRUCTURE NEEDS FROM THE PROGRAM, and the only two things it
// does. Below the transport this is the one place protocol identity may be
// written down (rule 4), and the program had never actually said either of
// them: the hand-written prog_id.c carries the number with a comment, and the
// configuration keys as two hand-written strcmps.
const uint8 TRANSPORT_IP_PROTO = 6;

// The configuration keys this protocol owns. The donor keeps these two in its
// own config struct; here the parser hands them up, because a timeout for a
// connection is not a property of the machine. An unrecognised key must not be
// silently ignored -- the configuration file the donor is measured with is the
// configuration file this target is measured with.
config transport { tcp_timewait; tcp_timeout; }

// §flow_id
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SHAPE ONLY (CR-5). The fields have no meaning here; the parser constructs the
// value and CANONICALISES direction so an inbound packet and an outbound
// application operation resolve the same context. That canonicalisation is
// protocol logic — only this program knows which half is local — and it is the
// piece the target cannot do for us.
flow_id tcp_fid : (uint32, uint32, uint16, uint16)

// A LISTENING ENDPOINT IS NOT NAMED BY A CONNECTION'S KEY. It has no remote
// half, so keying it on the four-tuple meant building (ip, 0, port, 0) and a
// zero key was then indistinguishable from a real one -- which is why the
// connection had to carry a `has_lst` flag beside the key to say whether it
// meant anything. Its own shape removes both problems: the key is well formed,
// and the two kinds cannot collide.
flow_id tcp_lid : (uint32, uint16)

// §events
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
event tcp_syn      : net_event { uint32 seq; uint16 sport, dport, window;
                                 uint32 ts_val; bool has_ts;
                                 uint8 wscale; bool has_wscale; }
event tcp_synack   : net_event { uint32 seq, ack; uint16 window;
                                 uint32 ts_val, ts_ecr; bool has_ts;
                                 uint8 wscale; bool has_wscale; }
event tcp_ack      : net_event { uint32 seq, ack; uint16 window;
                                 uint32 ts_val, ts_ecr; uint32 payload_len; }
event tcp_data     : net_event { uint32 seq, ack; uint16 window; uint32 ts_val;
                                 rx_addr_t hold_addr; uint32 data_len; }
event tcp_fin      : net_event { uint32 seq, ack; uint16 window; uint32 ts_val;
                                 uint32 payload_len; }
// A RESET RAISES tcp_rst AND NOTHING ELSE — the one place the inclusive
// classification in §net_parser does not apply. It carries the offending
// segment's own numbers because a reset answering a stranger is built from
// them, mirrored.
event tcp_rst      : net_event { uint32 seq, ack; uint16 sport, dport;
                                 uint32 payload_len; }

// ONE EVENT PER TIMER. No fields on any of them -- a timer identifies itself by
// which event it raises, which is why there is no timer id anywhere.
event tcp_rto_timeout      : timer_event { }
event tcp_timewait_timeout : timer_event { }
event tcp_probe_timeout    : timer_event { }
event tcp_idle_timeout     : timer_event { }

// The application side. OUT FIELDS: a processor writes one and the generated
// dispatch hands it back to the synchronous call that raised the event.
//
// `accepted` and `opened` are handles -- CR-2, already ruled on. `result` is
// the OTHER half of the same need and is not written down anywhere yet: a
// socket `read` answers with a BYTE COUNT, and 0 means end-of-stream while -1
// means "nothing now". Without it every read answers 0, every application sees
// end-of-stream on a connection that has only just opened, and no byte is ever
// served. See docs/LANGUAGE-NEEDS.md.
event app_bind     : app_event { uint32 ip; uint16 port; }
event app_listen   : app_event { uint32 ip; uint16 port; uint32 backlog;
                                 tcp_lid opened; }
event app_accept   : app_event { uint32 ip; uint16 port; tcp_fid accepted;
                                 int32 result; }
event app_connect  : app_event { uint32 loc_ip, rem_ip;
                                 uint16 loc_port, rem_port; tcp_fid opened; }
event app_recv     : app_event { rx_addr_t buf; uint32 len; int32 result; }
// TWO HALVES OF ONE EVENT ON TWO THREADS (CR-4). `record_data` copies into the
// transmit stream and generates nothing, so it runs inside the application's
// call; `gen_seg` advances send_next, which is the stack's. A compiler emits
// two entry points and the target calls each from the right thread; with one
// entry point in the contract the phase rides on the event.
// TWO EVENTS, NOT ONE WITH A FLAG. CR-4: `app_send -> { record_data, gen_seg }`
// is ONE chain that runs on TWO THREADS -- record_data copies into the transmit
// stream inside the application's call, and gen_seg advances send_next, which
// is the stack's. A single chain with each processor testing a flag runs BOTH
// halves on whichever thread called, which is the race the split exists to
// remove. So the OP-PARSER classifies, as a parser does, and the two halves are
// two chains.
event app_send     : app_event { addr_t addr; uint32 mlen;
                                 uint32 ip; uint16 port; int32 result; }
event app_gen      : app_event { uint32 mlen; int32 result; }
event app_close    : app_event { }

// §context
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
context tcp_ctx {
    // The target's handle for this flow, placed here by the target when it
    // creates the context. The compiler emits the context struct, so it can put
    // a target handle in it; the program passes it back to `pkt_gen` and
    // `notify` and never looks inside.
    
    uint8  state = ST_CLOSED;

    // The endpoints. The PASSIVE open never needed the addresses: every packet
    // it answers arrives with them, and pkt_gen takes the flow whose route the
    // target already resolved. An ACTIVE open has to build the first packet
    // with no packet to copy from, and a reset built from a context goes out
    // through the orphan path, which takes addresses rather than a flow.
    uint16 loc_port, rem_port;
    uint32 local_ip, remote_ip;

    // Our own copy of the key, so a timer that outlives the packet path can
    // still name the context to destroy (D-24). The field was declared for
    // exactly this and left unassigned once, and `del_ctx` then deleted a
    // zeroed key silently: every connection that reached TIME_WAIT leaked.
    tcp_fid key;

    // The endpoint that accepted this connection, BY KEY. Held as a key rather
    // than a reference because contexts are keyed. A zero key is not a valid
    // tcp_lid, so the key alone says whether there is a listener.
    tcp_lid lst_key;

    // --- send side, sequence space -------------------------------------
    // TWO SPACES, and conflating them is the fifth instance of the class in
    // docs/PLAN.md §3. The transmit unit's offsets start at 0. The sequence
    // space starts at the PARITY_ISN, and the SYN CONSUMES ONE, so the first data byte
    // is sequence PARITY_ISN+1. `snd_base` is that, and it is the only bridge between
    // the two — anything relating a unit offset to a sequence number goes
    // through it.
    uint32 snd_base;
    uint32 send_una, send_next, write_end;
    // The highest send_next ever reached. Emitting below it is a
    // retransmission, whoever asked: marking the CALLER did not work, because
    // the timeout rewinds and its own send is refused, so the re-emission
    // happens later from the acknowledgement path. The rewind and the re-send
    // are separated in time; the sequence position is the fact.
    uint32 send_high;

    uint32 send_wnd  = 65535;         // the peer's window, ALREADY SCALED
    uint8  snd_wscale;                // the shift the peer asked for on its SYN
    uint32 cwnd      = PARITY_INIT_CWND;
    uint32 ssthresh;

    // THE DONOR'S WINDOW-UPDATE AND DUPLICATE-ACKNOWLEDGEMENT STATE.
    //
    // snd_wl1/snd_wl2 are RFC 793's: the sequence and acknowledgement numbers
    // of the segment that last moved the peer's window. They decide whether a
    // segment updates the window at all, and the duplicate test then asks
    // whether the window's right edge MOVED -- which is why counting duplicates
    // needs them and why send_wnd could not simply be assigned on every
    // acknowledgement, as it was.
    uint32 snd_wl1, snd_wl2;
    // The window's right edge as it stood BEFORE this segment was allowed to
    // move it. The donor computes it at the top of ProcessACK (tcp_in.c:326)
    // and compares against it after the update, which is how "the advertised
    // window did not change" is expressed. Written by §proc_window, read by
    // §proc_fast_retransmit, meaningless outside one chain.
    uint32 right_wnd_edge;
    uint32 last_ack_seq;              // for the "old and unchanged" half
    uint32 dup_acks;

    // --- receive side, sequence space -----------------------------------
    // The receive side's bridge, named for symmetry with snd_base. The peer's
    // SYN consumes one, so the first data byte it sends is at PARITY_ISN+1, and
    // `delivered` is seeded HERE rather than at zero — otherwise §window_rule
    // subtracts a sequence number from a byte count. That is the same bug as
    // the send side's, in the same week, and the two were found separately
    // because neither bridge had a name. They do now.
    uint32 rcv_base;
    uint32 recv_next;                 // next in-order byte; the cumulative ack
    sliding_wnd rx_wnd;               // the receive window: what has arrived,
                                      // and where the holes are. THE WINDOW
                                      // TRACKS THE HOLES -- that is what the
                                      // primitive is for, so the program keeps
                                      // nothing beside it
    uint32 delivered;                 // what the application has taken,
                                      // accumulated from rx_flush_and_notify
    uint32 rcv_wnd = PARITY_INITIAL_WINDOW;  // §window_rule owns every write to this
    // D11: we advertised a zero window FIELD and owe the peer an update once
    // the application has drained. The donor's need_wnd_adv, and the test is on
    // the SCALED field rather than on rcv_wnd (tcp_out.c:304-309) — under a
    // shift of 7 the peer reads zero with up to 127 bytes still free.
    bool   need_wnd_adv;
    // HOW MANY ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS ARE OWED. The donor's sndvar->ack_cnt, with the
    // donor's two ways of adding to it (tcp_out.c:1088-1096). A COUNTER AND NOT
    // A FLAG, which is the whole point: with a bool, ACK_APIECE silently means
    // ACK_AGGREGATE, a burst of out-of-order segments produces one duplicate
    // where the donor produces one per segment, and those duplicates are what
    // drives the peer's fast retransmit.
    uint32 ack_cnt;
    bool   fin_consumed;              // the peer's FIN took a sequence number,
                                      // and it is NOT data: G14

    // --- the peer's timestamp, echoed in ours ---------------------------
    uint32 ts_recent;
    // PAWS runs only against a peer that uses timestamps, and the donor decides
    // that ONCE, from the SYN's options (tcp_util.c:47-51 sets saw_timestamp
    // and seeds ts_recent there). A peer that sent none is not PAWS-checked at
    // all -- so this is not an optimisation, it is the condition the check is
    // defined under.
    bool   saw_timestamp;
    // The probe's 500 ms is measured since OUR acknowledgement, and the donor
    // stamps this on EVERY segment carrying the ACK flag (tcp_out.c:293) — so
    // on a busy connection its gate is essentially never satisfied and the
    // probe fires only once the connection has gone quiet. Stamping it only
    // when a probe was emitted measured the interval between probes instead,
    // and probed where the donor would not.
    uint32 last_ack_sent_ms;

    // --- the retransmission estimator, from the donor --------------------
    // rto = (srtt >> 3) + rttvar, srtt held scaled by eight, NO FLOOR AND NO
    // CEILING — so on this testbed the effective timeout is about 3 ms, roughly
    // twenty times the measured round trip. A retransmission on an idle link is
    // therefore a real event. Reproduce, do not correct.
    uint32 srtt;
    uint32 mdev;                      // the donor's mean deviation
    uint32 rttvar;
    uint32 rto_ms;
    // A SEPARATE FLAG, not `rto_ms != 0`. The donor has no floor, so an
    // estimate of zero is legal and common on a link whose round trip is far
    // below the tick — and testing the value treats that legal zero as "no
    // estimate yet" and falls back to 500 ms for ever. The estimator then works
    // perfectly and changes nothing, which is what happened.
    bool   have_rtt;
    uint32 rtx_count;

    // --- teardown --------------------------------------------------------
    // A FIN is outstanding. It CONSUMES SEQUENCE SPACE AND CARRIES NO PAYLOAD,
    // so the wire-based unacknowledged test — emitted payload against
    // acknowledged payload — cannot see it, and without this the retransmission
    // timer is never armed for a FIN that is the only thing left.
    bool   fin_pending;
    uint32 fin_seq;
    // The application has closed its send direction. The peer's FIN closes the
    // peer's path only (D-20: the rule is per-path), so our FIN waits for this.
    // A one-shot server sets it when it hands over its object.
    bool   app_closed;

    // --- the data units, embedded ----------------------------------------
    // Embedded BY VALUE (D-19): the context owns the storage and
    // new_tx_ordered_data initialises it. The ring inside is still allocated
    // lazily on first write, which IS the donor's shape — mTCP takes its send
    // buffer from a pool on first send, not at accept.
    stream tx, rx;
    bool   tx_open, rx_open;

    // THREE TIMERS ON THE CONNECTION, THREE EVENTS, plus the idle reaper. One
    // `timer_t` per deadline, each bound at declaration to the event its expiry
    // raises (CR-6). One declared timer and four in the C was DEFERRED.md C2:
    // the C told them apart by comparing addresses, which is a question a
    // program should not be answering.
    //
    // The donor has three lists as well -- retransmission, TIME_WAIT and
    // connection-idle -- and parity is measured against the donor, so the count
    // follows it rather than the paper's single ack_timeout.
    timer_t rto_timer   -> tcp_rto_timeout;
    timer_t tw_timer    -> tcp_timewait_timeout;
    timer_t probe_timer -> tcp_probe_timeout;   // D-25 piece 2, the
                                                // window-BLOCKED probe
    timer_t idle_timer  -> tcp_idle_timeout;    // the donor keeps one ordered
                                                // list and walks it per tick; a
                                                // per-flow timer is a different
                                                // mechanism with the same
                                                // observable
}

// §tcp_listen_ctx
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SECOND CONTEXT TYPE. A listener is not a connection: it has no sequence
// numbers, no windows and no timers, and the events it answers are a SYN and
// the application's accept. Giving it its own context is what keeps every field
// in §context meaningful for every connection that has one -- the alternative,
// one context type with half its fields unused in the listening state, is where
// "which fields are live right now" stops being answerable.
//
// It is stored like any other context, under a key the PROGRAM builds from
// (ip, port). The target holds no listener table and no
// matching rule: "match on address AND port" is a statement about TCP (G8, and
// the donor matches on port alone, fhash.c:137-143, so a socket bound to one
// address answers for every address on its host), and protocol policy does not
// belong in target infrastructure. Encoding the choice as a KEY rather than as
// a table keeps it here, where a different protocol can choose differently.
//
// It was a single file-scope struct, so the program could hold exactly one
// listening socket (DEFERRED.md D4). There is no granularity keyword in MTP: a
// context is declared, there are as many instances as the protocol needs, and
// the endpoint being a FIELD is what makes several listeners possible with no
// new machinery.
context tcp_listen_ctx {
    // Its own name, so it can be handed to the application. Not a target
    // handle: the flow id is the identifier, and the compiler supplies
    // whatever the target needs from the context itself.
    tcp_lid key;


    uint32 local_ip;                  // network order, compared against the
                                      // arriving packet's destination
    uint16 local_port;
    uint8  state = ST_CLOSED;         // ST_CLOSED until listen(), then ST_LISTEN

    // THE ACCEPT BACKLOG (C3/C4). `pending_cap` is protocol state: a SYN
    // arriving with the queue full must be DROPPED — not reset, because the
    // donor resets a refused listener and an exhausted flow pool, and a full
    // accept queue is neither — and the cap is what makes that decision
    // expressible in the program rather than in the target.
    uint32   pending_cap;
    uint32   pending_n;
    tcp_fid  pending[PROG_MAX_BACKLOG];

    // The object the application has posted to serve, per listener rather than
    // per process. A one-shot server hands it over once and every accepted
    // connection receives it; that is what epserver does with a file, and it is
    // enough to drive bulk send. It arrives through the app interface as a SEND
    // op, not as something this program invented — the bytes are the
    // application's and the program only decides when they go.
    addr_t obj;
    uint32 obj_len;
}

// §scratchpad
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Per-dispatch working state: one instance per event, discarded when the chain
// ends. How one processor tells the next something without either owning it.
scratchpad_t tcp_scratch {
    uint32 acked;       // new bytes this acknowledgement retired
    uint32 delivered;   // bytes the application took
    // WHICH of the donor's two enqueue options this segment asks for, kept
    // apart all the way to the emission because they mean different things: see
    // `ack_cnt` in §context. ACK_NONE is "this segment owes nothing", which is
    // not the same as owing an aggregated one.
    uint8  ack_opt = ACK_NONE;
    // The donor does NOT attempt a send on a fourth-or-later duplicate: it
    // inflates cwnd and re-adds nothing to its send list. §gen_seg is the next
    // link in that chain and would otherwise attempt one, so the decision is
    // carried here rather than by gen_seg guessing.
    bool   no_send;
    // What §gen_seg put on the wire, read by §drain_owed_acks. It is the
    // donor's SendTCPPacket return value: every data packet carries the ACK
    // flag, so a packet sent is an acknowledgement that no longer needs its own
    // frame, and the count is what its caller subtracts from `ack_cnt`.
    uint32 pkts_sent;
}
//
// The emitted C carries one field this program does not declare, `ctx_dead`,
// and it is not protocol state: it is how `del_ctx` ends the chain it was
// issued from. Our target frees the context immediately, so every later
// processor in the chain would read freed memory. A target that deferred
// destruction to the end of the pass would not need it, which is exactly why it
// is the backend's and not the program's.

// §blueprint
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `data_off` is a HEADER-LENGTH FIELD THE COMPILER FILLS, in exactly the sense
// `checksum16_t` is a field the target fills. It was `uint8` and the program
// computed it, which meant the program did arithmetic over the compiler's own
// option layout — the one thing it cannot know and the compiler cannot help it
// get wrong. See docs/LANGUAGE-NEEDS.md.
pkt_bp TCPBP { uint16 src_port, dst_port; uint32 seq_no, ack_seq;
               hdrlen4_t data_off; uint8 flags; uint16 window;
               checksum16_t checksum; uint16 urg_ptr;
               tcp_options opts; data_t data; }

// The L3 fields the parser reads. Addresses are read RAW and treated as opaque
// network-order bytes for the flow id, so they round-trip identically at both
// ends; `tot_len` is deliberately not read, because the on-wire total length
// needs a byte swap the parser has no reason to perform when the target already
// hands over the transport length.
// Field names are the TARGET's, because this is its struct and not ours: a
// rename here would need a mapping table in the compiler, which is the sort of
// thing that is right once and wrong afterwards. Addresses are read RAW and
// treated as opaque network-order bytes for the flow id, so they round-trip
// identically at both ends.
net_header ipv4 { uint32 saddr, daddr; uint8 protocol; }

// Option layout, frozen from tcp_out.c:83-124,181-192 and MEASURED against the
// donor's wire (docs/RESULTS.md 2026-08-12). The ORDER differs from Linux's
// own, which is what makes the comparison a real check rather than a tautology.
//
//   SYN / SYN-ACK, exactly 20 bytes:  MSS NOP NOP Timestamp NOP WScale
//   every non-SYN, exactly 12 bytes:  NOP NOP Timestamp
//
// Written as an ordered construction at the two build sites rather than as a
// layout table, because the order IS the parity item and a table would let it
// be reordered without anything reading differently.
opt_t opt_mss    { uint8 kind = 2; uint8 opt_len = 4;  uint16 mss; }
opt_t opt_nop    { uint8 kind = 1; }
opt_t opt_ts     { uint8 kind = 8; uint8 opt_len = 10; uint32 ts_val, ts_ecr; }
opt_t opt_wscale { uint8 kind = 3; uint8 opt_len = 3;  uint8 wscale; }
opt_union tcp_opt { opt_mss, opt_nop, opt_ts, opt_wscale }
opt_list tcp_options { list<tcp_opt> opts; }

// §segmentation
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE RULE IS WRITTEN AT THE CALL, not declared apart from it. Every reference
// program does it that way -- `bp.data = unseg_data(addr, len, MSS, [...])` --
// and a free-standing `seg_rule` named in a `deploy` block was ours. The rule
// is an argument to the segmentation, so it belongs where the segmentation is.
//
// TCP's recurrence is PER BYTE, which is why it is safe under coalescing and
// under anything else that changes how a blueprint is split. A protocol whose
// recurrence is per PACKET is not: QUIC advances its packet number once per
// packet, pkt_gen does not report how it split, and the program's prediction is
// then wrong. Worth knowing before copying this line into another program.
//
// The call site is in §gen_seg.




// §sock_recv_apply
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RECOMPUTE POINT 2 — the application drained (mTCP api.c:1141). `delivered`
// advances by what the flush instruction REPORTED it handed over, so the
// accounting cannot drift from what the target actually gave the application.
//
// Generation is the stack's, so this only ASKS: the retry list runs the
// generate chain on the stack thread, and §gen_wnd_adv builds the update there.
void sock_recv(tcp_ctx ctx, uint32 delivered_now) {
    ctx.delivered = ctx.delivered + delivered_now;
ctx.rcv_wnd = PARITY_RCVBUF_SIZE - ((ctx.recv_next - ctx.delivered)
                  - ((ctx.fin_consumed && ctx.recv_next != ctx.delivered) ? 1 : 0));
    if (ctx.need_wnd_adv && ctx.rcv_wnd > PARITY_MSS_PAYLOAD)
        retry(ctx);
}


// §tcp_window_field
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What goes in the header's window field. Unscaled on a SYN, because window
// scaling is not in effect until the handshake completes; shifted thereafter.
// This is the whole of the observed sequence 14600 -> 14592 -> 2048.
//
// A VALUE ONE BELOW 2048 IS CORRECT, NOT AN OFF-BY-ONE. Between the merge and
// the application's read the bytes are held, so the window is PARITY_RCVBUF_SIZE minus what
// is held: one byte held advertises 2047. Observed on the wire 2026-08-13.
// Anyone "fixing" this to an unconditional 2048 would be removing the mechanism
// and would only find out from a trace diff.
uint16 tcp_window_field(tcp_ctx ctx, bool is_syn) {
    if (is_syn)
        return uint16(ctx.rcv_wnd);
    return uint16(ctx.rcv_wnd >> PARITY_WSCALE);
}

// §build_hdr
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ONE PLACE AN OUTBOUND HEADER IS BUILT, and it is a program function
// rather than compiler output because THREE PROTOCOL SIDE EFFECTS HANG OFF IT.
//
//   - every packet that acknowledges resets the window probe's clock. That is
//     the donor's rule (tcp_out.c:293) and was not ours: ours stamped it only
//     when a probe was emitted, so the gate measured the interval between
//     probes and we probed where the donor would not;
//   - the same line stamps the donor's activity clock (tcp_out.c:293-296, and
//     again on every received packet at tcp_in.c:1292), which is what our idle
//     timer restart stands in for;
//   - D11 ARM. The donor sets need_wnd_adv when the SCALED FIELD it is about to
//     put on the wire is zero (tcp_out.c:304-309) — not when rcv_wnd is small.
//     Under a shift of 7 that happens with up to 127 bytes still free, which is
//     why the test is on the field and not on the window.
//
// Here rather than at the seven emission sites because there is exactly one
// header built per emission, and a rule enforced at seven call sites is a rule
// the eighth will not follow.
TCPBP build_hdr(tcp_ctx ctx, uint32 seq, uint8 flags, uint32 now) {
    TCPBP bp;
    bool  is_syn = (flags & FLAG_SYN) != 0;

    if ((flags & FLAG_ACK) != 0) {
        ctx.last_ack_sent_ms = now;
        // the idle deadline, refreshed; nothing to reap once closed
        if (ctx.state != ST_CLOSED && ctx.state != ST_TIME_WAIT)
            timer_start(ctx.idle_timer, millisecond(PARITY_IDLE_MS));
    }

    bp.src_port = ctx.loc_port;
    bp.dst_port = ctx.rem_port;
    bp.seq_no   = seq;
    bp.ack_seq  = ctx.recv_next;
    bp.flags    = PARITY_SET_PSH ? flags : (flags & ~FLAG_PSH);
    bp.window   = tcp_window_field(ctx, is_syn);
    bp.urg_ptr  = 0;

    if (bp.window == 0)
        ctx.need_wnd_adv = true;

    // The donor's order, and it is the parity item. Do not sort these.
    if (is_syn) {
        bp.opts.add(opt_mss(PARITY_MSS_ADVERTISED));
        bp.opts.add(opt_nop());
        bp.opts.add(opt_nop());
        bp.opts.add(opt_ts(now, ctx.ts_recent));
        bp.opts.add(opt_nop());
        bp.opts.add(opt_wscale(PARITY_WSCALE));
    } else {
        bp.opts.add(opt_nop());
        bp.opts.add(opt_nop());
        bp.opts.add(opt_ts(now, ctx.ts_recent));
    }
    return bp;
}

// §build_rst_hdr
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A reset's header, built WITHOUT A CONTEXT, because a reset for a connection
// that does not exist has none. Ports come from the offending segment with the
// halves swapped, and there are NO OPTIONS: the donor's SendTCPPacketStandalone
// sends none on this path, and a timestamp echo would need a `ts_recent` we
// have never had. The window is zero, because there is no receive buffer behind
// a connection that does not exist, and the donor passes 0 at every standalone
// site.
TCPBP build_rst_hdr(uint16 loc_port, uint16 rem_port, uint32 seq, uint32 ack,
                    uint8 flags) {
    TCPBP bp;

    bp.src_port = loc_port;
    bp.dst_port = rem_port;
    bp.seq_no   = seq;
    bp.ack_seq  = ack;
    bp.flags    = flags;
    bp.window   = 0;
    bp.urg_ptr  = 0;
    return bp;
}





// §unacked_on_wire
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Is there data ON THE WIRE that has not been acknowledged? The only thing a
// retransmission timer should be waiting for.
//
// NOT `send_una != send_next`: send_next advances at GENERATION, so that test
// is true for a blueprint still sitting in the target's ring. Arming on it lets
// the timer expire against data that never went out, and the expiry rewinds
// send_next and regenerates — producing a retransmission for a range a live
// blueprint is still carrying, whose reference can then never drain.
//
// THE FIN. "Emitted payload beyond what is acknowledged" cannot see a FIN,
// which emits none — so once the data is all acknowledged this test went false,
// the timer was stopped, and a lost FIN was never retransmitted. The second
// clause is that case, and it is the reason this is not one expression.
// THE EMITTED C CARRIES A RUNTIME TOGGLE HERE (MTP_RTO_ARM_ON_GENERATION),
// which restores the generation-based test so both arms of the comparison come
// from ONE binary. It is EXPERIMENT SCAFFOLDING, not protocol: this program
// describes the default arm, which is the one below.
bool unacked_on_wire(tcp_ctx ctx) {
    uint64 acked = ctx.send_una - ctx.snd_base;

    if (ctx.tx_open && tx_emitted(ctx.tx) > acked)
        return true;
    return ctx.fin_pending && ctx.send_una != ctx.send_next;
}


// §estimate_rtt
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The round-trip estimator, from the donor: rto = (srtt >> 3) + rttvar, with
// srtt held scaled by eight. NO FLOOR AND NO CEILING — reproduce, do not
// correct. The sample comes from the timestamp the peer echoes, which is why
// this program sends timestamps on every segment.
//
// A SAMPLE OF AT LEAST ONE TICK, which is what the donor's recorded state says
// it takes: the donor sits at srtt = 8, rttvar = 2, which is exactly this
// function's first-sample initialisation with m = 1, not m = 0. The round trip
// here is at most 0.229 ms, so a same-tick echo gives m = 0 and a timeout of 0
// that the wheel floors at one tick — a THIRD of the donor's three, and we
// retransmit on an idle link where it does not. This is a floor on the SAMPLE,
// not on the RTO; the RTO still has none.
//
// AND IT IS THE DONOR'S COMPLETE CHECK: `if (m == 0) m = 1;` is all it does. No
// bound, no ahead-of-clock test, no Karn's rule. So we add no validation the
// donor lacks.
//
// THE FORM MATTERS, because the next reader will think `mdev` is an
// idiosyncratic way of writing the textbook estimator and simplify it:
//
//     textbook   rttvar += (|err| - rttvar) >> 2     decays
//     donor      mdev   += |err| - (mdev >> 2)       pinned
//
// Same intent, same shape, opposite behaviour. With m == 1, which is what this
// testbed always produces, srtt = 8, mdev = 2, RTO = 3 ticks and it DOES NOT
// MOVE — rttvar cannot decay because `mdev >> 2` is 0 at mdev = 2. One 2-tick
// sample takes the timeout to 4 ticks permanently. At these magnitudes the
// donor's timeout is monotonically non-decreasing, and an estimator that decays
// properly sits at 3 where the donor sits at 4, so the timers fire at different
// moments. Only running both found this.
void estimate_rtt(tcp_ctx ctx, uint32 now, uint32 ts_ecr) {
    uint32 m;

    if (ts_ecr == 0)
        return;
    m = now - ts_ecr;                // in 1 ms ticks
    if (m == 0)
        m = 1;

    if (ctx.srtt == 0) {
        ctx.srtt = m << 3;
        ctx.mdev = m << 1;
    } else {
        int32 d = int32(m) - int32(ctx.srtt >> 3);

        ctx.srtt = uint32(int32(ctx.srtt) + d);
        if (d < 0) {
            d = -d;
            d = d - int32(ctx.mdev >> 2);
            if (d > 0)
                d = d >> 3;
        } else {
            d = d - int32(ctx.mdev >> 2);
        }
        ctx.mdev = uint32(int32(ctx.mdev) + d);
    }
    ctx.rttvar = ctx.mdev;
    ctx.rto_ms = (ctx.srtt >> 3) + ctx.rttvar;
    ctx.have_rtt = true;
}

// §net_parser
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// parse_tcp : a segment -> the list of events it raises.
//
// INCLUSIVE, and that is the donor's shape rather than a convenience. mTCP's
// Handle_TCP_ST_ESTABLISHED runs three independent tests on one packet --
// payload, then acknowledgement, then FIN -- so a segment carrying all three
// does all three jobs there. One event per segment would drop the payload of a
// FIN that carries data, and would fail to establish a connection whose
// handshake-completing acknowledgement carries the request.
//
//   RST                   -> tcp_rst        (alone: the donor returns from
//                                            ProcessTCPPacket on a true result,
//                                            so no payload is delivered and no
//                                            acknowledgement is retired)
//   SYN, no ACK           -> tcp_syn        (alone: nothing else is in sequence)
//   SYN + ACK             -> tcp_synack     (alone, likewise)
//   ACK                   -> tcp_ack
//   payload               -> tcp_data
//   FIN                   -> tcp_fin
//
// The last three compose. ORDER IS acknowledgement, payload, FIN -- the reverse
// of the donor's internal order, which is not observable because it defers both
// its acknowledgement and its send to the end of the pass. Ours is ordered this
// way because §proc_congestion is in the tcp_ack chain and reads
// `ack - send_una`: let tcp_data's chain retire the bytes first and the
// congestion window sees nothing newly acknowledged and stops growing.
//
// THE PARSER ISSUES INSTRUCTIONS AND RESOLVES CONTEXTS, which the frozen
// language does not say it may (docs/LANGUAGE-NEEDS.md N-E). It has to: the
// three jobs below are all protocol policy and none of them can be done from a
// chain.
//
//   a segment for a connection that does not exist is answered with a RESET,
//   and a chain is dispatched THROUGH a context — there is none to dispatch
//   through (§gen_rst);
//
//   a SYN is matched against a LISTENER, and whether the backlog has room
//   decides whether a context is created at all (G8, C3);
//
//   §proc_validate refuses a segment BEFORE any event is raised, which is the
//   donor's placement — ProcessTCPPacket calls ValidateSequence after the flow
//   lookup and before the state handlers, and drops on a false return. Running
//   it as the first link of every chain instead would validate a segment that
//   raises two events TWICE, and the second ACK_APIECE would owe a duplicate
//   acknowledgement the donor does not send.
list<event_t> parse_tcp(pkt_t p, ipv4 h) {
    list<event_t> out;

    TCPBP  bp = extract(p);
    uint32 payload_len;

    // A MALFORMED SEGMENT RAISES NOTHING. Shorter than a bare header, or a
    // stated header length that is below the minimum or runs past the bytes we
    // were handed: there is nothing to classify and no context to answer from.
    if (p.len < 20 || bp.data_off * 4 < 20 || bp.data_off * 4 > p.len)
        return out;
    payload_len = p.len - bp.data_off * 4;
    tcp_fid fid = tcp_fid(h.daddr, h.saddr, bp.dst_port, bp.src_port);

    tcp_ctx ctx = ctx_lookup(fid);

    if (!exists(ctx)) {
        // A segment for a connection that does not exist. tcp_in.c:744/748 --
        // the donor answers with a reset rather than dropping it, which is what
        // lets a peer holding a stale connection find out.
        if ((bp.flags & FLAG_SYN) == 0 || (bp.flags & FLAG_ACK) != 0) {
            gen_rst(h.daddr, h.saddr, bp, payload_len);
            return out;
        }

        tcp_fid lk = tcp_lid(h.daddr, bp.dst_port);
        tcp_listen_ctx lst = ctx_lookup(lk);

        // tcp_in.c:700 -- a SYN the listener filter refuses is answered with a
        // reset. A MISS IS A MISS, never a null context handed onward, which is
        // how the prototype turns a missed lookup into a crash.
        if (!exists(lst) || lst.state != ST_LISTEN) {
            gen_rst(h.daddr, h.saddr, bp, payload_len);
            return out;
        }

        // C3: A SYN ARRIVING WITH THE QUEUE FULL IS DROPPED, and dropped here --
        // before any context exists, so nothing is allocated for a connection
        // that will not be accepted. The peer retransmits its SYN and finds room
        // later, which is the behaviour a backlog is for. It does NOT reset: the
        // donor resets a refused listener and an exhausted flow pool, and a full
        // accept queue is neither.
        if (lst.pending_n >= lst.pending_cap)
            return out;

        ctx = new_ctx(fid);
        ctx.key      = fid;
        ctx.lst_key  = lk;
        ctx.loc_port = bp.dst_port;
        ctx.rem_port = bp.src_port;
        ctx.local_ip = h.daddr;
        ctx.remote_ip = h.saddr;

        tcp_syn s;
        s.seq = bp.seq_no;  s.sport = bp.src_port;  s.dport = bp.dst_port;
        s.window = bp.window;
        s.ts_val = bp.opts.ts_val;      s.has_ts = bp.opts.has_ts;
        s.wscale = bp.opts.wscale;      s.has_wscale = bp.opts.has_wscale;
        set_flow_id(s, fid);
        out.add(s);
        return out;
    }

    // tcp_in.c:1292 -- the donor stamps activity on EVERY received packet,
    // before validation and before any state test.
    // the idle deadline, refreshed; nothing to reap once closed
    if (ctx.state != ST_CLOSED && ctx.state != ST_TIME_WAIT)
        timer_start(ctx.idle_timer, millisecond(PARITY_IDLE_MS));

    if (!proc_validate(ctx, bp, payload_len))
        return out;             // refused: it raises nothing, and whatever
                                // acknowledgement the donor queues on that path
                                // has already been queued by proc_validate

    if ((bp.flags & FLAG_RST) != 0) {
        tcp_rst r;
        r.seq = bp.seq_no;  r.ack = bp.ack_seq;
        r.sport = bp.src_port;  r.dport = bp.dst_port;
        r.payload_len = payload_len;
        set_flow_id(r, fid);
        out.add(r);
        return out;
    }

    if ((bp.flags & FLAG_SYN) != 0) {
        // A handshake segment raises one event and nothing else: no payload of
        // ours is in sequence yet, and the acknowledgement it may carry is the
        // handshake's own. A bare SYN on a context that already exists is a
        // RETRANSMITTED SYN — the passive open handled the first one and there
        // is nothing here to do with the second.
        if ((bp.flags & FLAG_ACK) != 0) {
            tcp_synack sa;
            sa.seq = bp.seq_no;  sa.ack = bp.ack_seq;  sa.window = bp.window;
            sa.ts_val = bp.opts.ts_val;  sa.ts_ecr = bp.opts.ts_ecr;
            sa.has_ts = bp.opts.has_ts;
            sa.wscale = bp.opts.wscale;  sa.has_wscale = bp.opts.has_wscale;
            set_flow_id(sa, fid);
            out.add(sa);
        }
        return out;
    }

    if ((bp.flags & FLAG_ACK) != 0) {
        tcp_ack a;
        a.seq = bp.seq_no;  a.ack = bp.ack_seq;  a.window = bp.window;
        a.ts_val = bp.opts.ts_val;  a.ts_ecr = bp.opts.ts_ecr;
        a.payload_len = payload_len;
        set_flow_id(a, fid);
        out.add(a);
    }
    if (payload_len > 0) {
        tcp_data d;
        d.seq = bp.seq_no;  d.ack = bp.ack_seq;  d.window = bp.window;
        d.ts_val = bp.opts.ts_val;
        d.hold_addr = bp.data;  d.data_len = payload_len;
        set_flow_id(d, fid);
        out.add(d);
    }
    if ((bp.flags & FLAG_FIN) != 0) {
        tcp_fin f;
        f.seq = bp.seq_no;  f.ack = bp.ack_seq;  f.window = bp.window;
        f.ts_val = bp.opts.ts_val;  f.payload_len = payload_len;
        set_flow_id(f, fid);
        out.add(f);
    }
    return out;
}

// §app_parser
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CR-7. The target defines the op schema (docs/DESIGN.md §17.1) and this
// program maps the ops it needs. There is no universal `send`.
//
// `recv` IS BOUND, and the example kernel program does not bind it. Parity
// requires it: the donor recomputes its advertised window when the application
// drains (api.c:1141), and a program never told the application drained cannot
// reproduce the window-reopen acknowledgement. Without it a connection that
// reaches a zero window has nothing to restart it. `recv` is an event like any
// other; there is no special category and nothing to relax.
app_parser socket {
    bind    -> sock_bind;
    listen  -> sock_listen;
    accept  -> sock_accept;
    connect -> sock_connect;
    send    -> sock_send;
    recv    -> parse_recv;
    close   -> sock_close;
}

// The op's endpoint is in NETWORK order, as the schema declares; this program
// works in host order once past the parser. Converting HERE rather than at the
// comparison site keeps the one representation choice in one place — the two
// being out of step is what made the listener never match and swallowed every
// SYN silently.
list<event_t> sock_bind(bind op) {
    list<event_t> out;
    app_bind ev;

    ev.ip = op.local.ip;                 // stays network order: compared against
                                         // the arriving packet's destination
    ev.port = ntohs(op.local.port);
    set_flow_id(ev, tcp_lid(op.local.ip, ntohs(op.local.port)));
    out.add(ev);
    return out;
}

list<event_t> sock_listen(listen op) {
    list<event_t> out;
    app_listen ev;

    ev.ip = op.local.ip;
    ev.port = ntohs(op.local.port);
    ev.backlog = op.len;
    set_flow_id(ev, tcp_lid(op.local.ip, ntohs(op.local.port)));
    out.add(ev);
    return out;
}

list<event_t> sock_accept(accept op) {
    list<event_t> out;
    app_accept ev;

    ev.ip = op.local.ip;
    ev.port = ntohs(op.local.port);
    set_flow_id(ev, tcp_lid(op.local.ip, ntohs(op.local.port)));
    out.add(ev);
    return out;
}

// THE LOCAL PORT IS THE CALLER'S (B4): the donor allocates from a per-core
// address pool in mtcp_connect and fails EAGAIN when exhausted, and whose job
// that is in general is undecided — it is protocol policy in TCP and not
// obviously so elsewhere. The op carries both endpoints for that reason.
list<event_t> sock_connect(connect op) {
    list<event_t> out;
    app_connect ev;

    ev.loc_ip = op.local.ip;    ev.rem_ip = op.remote.ip;
    ev.loc_port = ntohs(op.local.port);
    ev.rem_port = ntohs(op.remote.port);
    set_flow_id(ev, tcp_fid(op.local.ip, op.remote.ip,
                                   ntohs(op.local.port),
                                   ntohs(op.remote.port)));
    out.add(ev);
    return out;
}

// TWO HALVES ON TWO THREADS (CR-4). The phase rides on the op because the
// contract has one entry point; the program never branches on a thread, and the
// generated dispatch branches on which half it was asked for, which is
// knowledge the compiler has because it made the placement.
//
// A send with NO FLOW is the object posted before any connection exists, which
// is how a one-shot server hands over what every accepted connection receives.
// It is posted to a LISTENER, not to the process: with several listeners there
// is no "the" listener to post to, which is why the op carries the endpoint.
list<event_t> sock_send(send op) {
    list<event_t> out;

    // WHICH HALF, decided here. A send that NAMES A FLOW attaches the handle
    // the socket layer already holds; one that does not is the posted object,
    // and keys a LISTENER.
    if ((op.flags & OP_PHASE_RECORD) != 0) {
        app_send ev;

        ev.addr = op.data;
        ev.mlen = op.len;
        ev.ip = op.local.ip;
        ev.port = ntohs(op.local.port);
        if (has_flow(op))
            set_flow(ev, op.flow);
        else
            set_flow_id(ev, tcp_lid(op.local.ip, ntohs(op.local.port)));
        out.add(ev);
    } else {
        app_gen ev;

        ev.mlen = op.len;
        set_flow(ev, op.flow);
        out.add(ev);
    }
    return out;
}

// §sock_recv
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The application drains. RECOMPUTE POINT 2 of §window_rule and the only reason
// `recv` is bound at all: `delivered` advances by what the flush instruction
// REPORTS it handed over — not by what the application asked for, which may be
// more — and the advertised window reopens by exactly that much. The count
// comes from the instruction's return value rather than from a target accessor,
// which is what lets the program own `delivered` without the target exposing
// its unit's occupancy. The work itself is §proc_drain.
list<event_t> parse_recv(recv op) {
    list<event_t> out;
    app_recv ev;

    // The op schema's `data` is a TRANSMIT reference; a receive posts a
    // RECEIVE one. The two are different types in this target's contract, so
    // the program says which rather than the compiler converting silently.
    ev.buf = rx_addr(op.data.base, op.len);
    ev.len = op.len;
    set_flow(ev, op.flow);
    out.add(ev);
    return out;
}

// §sock_close
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The application has no more to send. THIS, AND NOT THE PEER'S FIN, IS WHAT
// RELEASES OUR OWN FIN.
//
// D-20's per-path rule reaches teardown here. A peer's FIN closes the PEER's
// path; ours stays open until this arrives, so CLOSE_WAIT is a state in which
// the program still accepts writes, still generates segments, and still
// processes returning acknowledgements. Gating any of those three on
// ESTABLISHED alone silently discards a response to a request whose FIN arrived
// with it — which is a request/response server's ordinary case, and was
// invisible for as long as the object was posted before any connection existed.
//
// §gen_fin is tried immediately rather than waiting for the next packet,
// because if the peer has already finished, no further packet will arrive to
// carry the attempt.
list<event_t> sock_close(close op) {
    list<event_t> out;
    app_close ev;

    set_flow(ev, op.flow);
    out.add(ev);
    return out;
}

// §proc_validate
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PAWS and the sequence-acceptability test, run BEFORE any event is raised.
//
// It is the donor's ValidateSequence, reproduced rather than re-derived, and
// three things about it are worth stating because a standards-based
// implementation would differ:
//
//   - the acceptability test is on `seq + payload_len` lying inside
//     [recv_next, recv_next + rcv_wnd]. RFC 793's test is on the segment's
//     first and last bytes separately; this is the donor's. It is the reason a
//     segment can be refused whose FIRST byte was inside the window — the right
//     edge moves left as unread data piles up, so data the peer sent
//     legitimately can arrive after the edge has retreated behind it. Measured
//     2026-08-26: 68,110 of 8,123,072 segments. The donor does the same thing
//     and drops fewer only because its application leaves less unread;
//   - a segment from a timestamping peer that carries NO timestamp is DROPPED.
//     The donor leaves a TODO where a standards-based handler would go;
//   - `ts_recent` is updated HERE, before dispatch, and only for a segment this
//     accepts. Updating it in §proc_timestamp for any acknowledgement moved it
//     on segments the donor would have rejected.
//
// A REFUSAL IS NOT SILENT: PAWS failure and a segment outside the window both
// queue an acknowledgement, so the peer learns where we are. A reset outside
// the window is ignored instead, and answered by nothing.
//
// THE STATE GUARD IS WRITTEN AS NAMES, NOT AS AN ORDERING. The donor guards the
// whole call with `state > TCP_ST_SYN_RCVD`, and in ITS enum SYN_SENT sits
// BELOW SYN_RCVD, so that one test excludes both halves of a handshake. Ours
// appended SYN_SENT at the END of the state list, because it was added last, so
// the same comparison would have included it. It cost a day: an active open
// validated its own SYN-ACK against a recv_next of zero, rejected it, and the
// connection never completed.
bool proc_validate(tcp_ctx ctx, TCPBP bp, uint32 payload_len) {
    uint32 seg_end;

    if (ctx.state == ST_CLOSED || ctx.state == ST_LISTEN ||
        ctx.state == ST_SYN_RCVD || ctx.state == ST_SYN_SENT)
        return true;

    // --- PAWS ----------------------------------------------------------
    if ((bp.flags & FLAG_RST) == 0 && ctx.saw_timestamp) {
        if (!bp.opts.has_ts)
            return false;               // no timestamp from a peer that
                                        // timestamps: dropped
        if (int32(bp.opts.ts_val - ctx.ts_recent) < 0) {
            // RFC 1323: SEG.TSval < TS.Recent -> drop, and acknowledge so the
            // peer learns where we are.
            owe_ack(ctx, ACK_APIECE);
            return false;
        }
        ctx.ts_recent = bp.opts.ts_val;
    }

    // --- sequence acceptability -----------------------------------------
    seg_end = bp.seq_no + payload_len;
    if (int32(seg_end - ctx.recv_next) >= 0 &&
        int32(seg_end - (ctx.recv_next + ctx.rcv_wnd)) <= 0)
        return true;                    // acceptable

    if ((bp.flags & FLAG_RST) != 0)
        return false;                   // a reset outside the window is
                                        // ignored, not answered

    if (ctx.state == ST_ESTABLISHED) {
        // THREE CASES, and the donor answers them differently. We answered all
        // three the same way, which is the collapse `ack_cnt` exists to undo.
        //
        //   the peer's WINDOW PROBE -- one byte behind what we expect next, so
        //   deliberately outside our window and unmistakable -- and anything at
        //   or behind the boundary: AGGREGATE. Both are the peer asking where
        //   we are, and one answer per pass tells it.
        //
        //   anything AHEAD of the window: one apiece. These are the segments a
        //   sender keeps pushing past a hole, and each owes its own duplicate
        //   for the same reason as in §proc_recv.
        //
        // The probe is answered HERE rather than in §proc_recv because the donor
        // answers it from inside validation and then STOPS PROCESSING the
        // segment. D-25 piece 3: not optional, and easy to miss because it is
        // receive-side work that exists only to serve the other end's send-side
        // mechanism. Without it the donor as sender never learns our window
        // reopened and the stall simply moves to the other end of the
        // connection.
        if (bp.seq_no + 1 == ctx.recv_next)
            owe_ack(ctx, ACK_AGGREGATE);
        else if (int32(bp.seq_no - ctx.recv_next) <= 0)
            owe_ack(ctx, ACK_AGGREGATE);
        else
            owe_ack(ctx, ACK_APIECE);
    } else {
        // Outside ESTABLISHED the donor puts the stream on its CONTROL list,
        // not its acknowledgement list, and refreshes the TIME_WAIT deadline if
        // that is where it is. We have no separate control path, so this
        // becomes one apiece: the nearest thing this program has, and the
        // difference is recorded rather than hidden (docs/DIVERGENCE.md).
        if (ctx.state == ST_TIME_WAIT)
            ctx.state = ST_TIME_WAIT;
        timer_start(ctx.tw_timer, millisecond(PARITY_TIMEWAIT_MS));
        owe_ack(ctx, ACK_APIECE);
    }
    return false;
}

// §gen_rst
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Answer a segment that belongs to no connection.
//
// THE DONOR SENDS A RESET ONLY HERE. All six of its emission sites are in
// ProcessTCPPacket, for a segment with no live stream, via
// SendTCPPacketStandalone. It never sends one from an established connection
// and mtcp_close always sends a FIN — SO AN ABORTIVE CLOSE HAS NO
// REPRESENTATION IN THE DONOR EITHER, and adding one here would be a divergence
// rather than parity.
//
// It is the one generation path with NO CONTEXT to generate from, which is why
// it is in no chain: a chain is dispatched through a context and there is none.
// It is also the reason the target has an orphan emit path at all.
//
// Two shapes, and which one depends on whether the offending segment carried an
// acknowledgement (tcp_in.c:744 and :748):
//
//   with ACK: seq = its ack, no ACK flag of our own, no acknowledgement number
//   without:  seq = 0, ACK flag set, ack = its seq + its length (+1 for a SYN)
//
// The second form has to acknowledge something because a bare reset with
// sequence zero would be discarded by any receiver checking the window.
void gen_rst(uint32 local_ip, uint32 remote_ip, TCPBP seg,
             uint32 payload_len) {
    uint32 seq, ack;
    uint8  flags;

    if ((seg.flags & FLAG_RST) != 0)
        return;                  // never answer a reset with a reset

    if ((seg.flags & FLAG_ACK) != 0) {
        seq = seg.ack_seq;
        ack = 0;
        flags = FLAG_RST;
    } else {
        seq = 0;
        ack = seg.seq_no + payload_len + ((seg.flags & FLAG_SYN) != 0 ? 1 : 0);
        flags = FLAG_RST | FLAG_ACK;
    }

    pkt_gen_orphan(local_ip, remote_ip,
                           build_rst_hdr(seg.dst_port, seg.src_port,
                                         seq, ack, flags));
    return;
}

// §gen_rst_from_ctx
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A reset for a connection we DO have a context for — the one case the donor
// resets from a stream it knows about (Handle_TCP_ST_SYN_SENT, an unacceptable
// acknowledgement). It still goes out through the orphan path, because the
// reset carries the offending segment's numbers rather than ours.
void gen_rst_from_ctx(tcp_ctx ctx, uint32 ack) {
    pkt_gen_orphan(ctx.local_ip, ctx.remote_ip,
                           build_rst_hdr(ctx.loc_port, ctx.rem_port,
                                         ack, 0, FLAG_RST));
    return;
}

// §proc_passive_open
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A SYN with no context, and a listener that matches. The parser created the
// context and recorded the endpoints; everything the handshake decides is here.
void proc_passive_open(tcp_syn ev, tcp_ctx ctx) {
    ctx.state = ST_SYN_RCVD;
    ctx.recv_next = ev.seq + 1;         // the SYN consumes one byte

    // `delivered` is the SAME ORIGIN as recv_next, not zero. §window_rule
    // computes rcv_wnd = PARITY_RCVBUF_SIZE - (recv_next - delivered), and that difference
    // has to be a BYTE COUNT. recv_next is an absolute sequence number seeded
    // from the peer's PARITY_ISN, so leaving delivered at zero makes the difference a
    // sequence number and the window arbitrary — it advertised 29423 on the
    // first acknowledgement here. Fourth instance of the units bug this
    // increment (docs/PLAN.md §3): two quantities of the same type, one
    // absolute and one relative, subtracted, with nothing to type-check it.
    ctx.rcv_base  = ctx.recv_next;
    ctx.delivered = ctx.rcv_base;
    sw_init(ctx.rx_wnd, ctx.recv_next);

    // PAWS applies only to a peer that timestamps, and the donor decides it
    // ONCE, here, from the SYN's options (tcp_util.c:47-51). ts_recent is
    // seeded at the same moment and by the same line there.
    ctx.saw_timestamp = ev.has_ts;
    ctx.ts_recent = ev.ts_val;

    // The peer's window scale, from ITS SYN. It applies to every window it
    // advertises AFTER the handshake and not to the SYN's own field, which is
    // unscaled — so this records the shift and the SYN's window is taken raw.
    // Reading a later window raw sent 502 bytes where the peer had offered
    // 64256 and the congestion window should have bounded the send at 2920,
    // which is a throughput difference with no visible cause.
    ctx.snd_wscale = ev.has_wscale ? ev.wscale : 0;
    ctx.send_wnd = ev.window;

    // D-01, behind PARITY_OPENING_TRAJECTORY. mTCP never assigns ssthresh on
    // the passive-open path, so the server runs with ssthresh == 0, the
    // slow-start test is false from the first acknowledgement, and it does
    // congestion avoidance from cwnd = 2*MSS. Reproduced knowingly.
    ctx.send_una  = PARITY_ISN;
    ctx.send_next = PARITY_ISN;
    ctx.cwnd      = PARITY_INIT_CWND;
    ctx.ssthresh  = PARITY_OPENING_TRAJECTORY ? PARITY_SSTHRESH_PASSIVE
                                              : PARITY_SSTHRESH_ACTIVE;

    if (pkt_gen(ctx, build_hdr(ctx, ctx.send_next, FLAG_SYN | FLAG_ACK,
                                 now()), PRIO_CONTROL, false) == 0) {
        ctx.send_next = ctx.send_next + 1;   // the SYN-ACK consumes one
        ctx.snd_base  = ctx.send_next;       // ...so data starts one past
        // CR-E: the transmit stream exists from ESTABLISHMENT, not from the
        // first write. The target buffers into it on the application thread and
        // needs the unit valid before the application's first write, which a
        // lazy open cannot promise.
        if (!ctx.tx_open) {
            new_tx_ordered_data(ctx.tx, INF);
            ctx.tx_open = true;
        }
    }
    return;
}

// §proc_open_done
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The acknowledgement that completes a passive open.
void proc_open_done(tcp_ack ev, tcp_ctx ctx) {
    if (ctx.state != ST_SYN_RCVD)
        return;
    if (ev.ack != ctx.send_next)
        return;

    ctx.send_una = ev.ack;
    ctx.send_wnd = uint32(ev.window) << ctx.snd_wscale;
    ctx.ts_recent = ev.ts_val;
    ctx.state = ST_ESTABLISHED;

    // The emitted C carries a runtime toggle here (MTP_LEGACY_OPEN_NOTIFY)
    // restoring a STATE notification on the CONNECTION with no queue and no
    // listener event. It is EXPERIMENT SCAFFOLDING -- it exists because moving
    // to the donor's channel coincided with an 8.7x change in completions at
    // c=1 that nothing in the counters explains, and an unexplained factor of
    // eight is not something to build on. This program describes the default
    // arm, below.
    //
    // D9: READABLE ON THE LISTENING CONTEXT, which is what makes accept()
    // return. Raising a STATE notification on the CONNECTION is neither what
    // the donor does nor something an accepting application can act on — the
    // donor enqueues the stream on the accept queue (tcp_in.c:884) and raises
    // EPOLLIN on the listening socket (:898-900).
    // No `has_lst` test: a connection that never had a listener holds a
    // zero key, which is not a valid tcp_lid, so the lookup misses and
    // `exists` below is false. The flag existed only because a zero
    // four-tuple was a key like any other.
    tcp_listen_ctx lst = ctx_lookup(ctx.lst_key);

    if (exists(lst) && lst.pending_n < PROG_MAX_BACKLOG) {
        lst.pending[lst.pending_n] = ctx.key;
        lst.pending_n = lst.pending_n + 1;
        notify(lst, READABLE);
    }
    // The application posted an object before listening; serve it. It
    // arrives through the app interface as a SEND, so this is the same two
    // steps the application thread takes — buffer, then hand over the
    // extent — except that this path already runs on the stack thread and
    // so does both itself.
    if (exists(lst) && lst.obj_len > 0) {
        uint32 wrote = add_tx_data(ctx.tx, lst.obj, lst.obj_len);

        if (wrote > 0) {
            tcp_scratch gs;

            ctx.write_end = ctx.write_end + wrote;
            // The generate half, run inline: this path is ALREADY on the
            // stack thread, so it does both steps itself rather than
            // buffering and asking. Same three processors, same order, as
            // the app_send generate chain.
            gen_seg(ev, ctx, gs);
            drain_owed_acks(ev, ctx, gs);
            gen_wnd_adv(ev, ctx);
        }
        ctx.app_closed = true;   // a one-shot server has said all it will
    }
    return;
}

// §proc_timestamp
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Record the peer's echo for our own.
//
// ONLY FOR A PEER THAT DOES NOT TIMESTAMP. For one that does, §proc_validate
// has already set it — before dispatch, and only for a segment it accepted,
// which is the donor's placement.
void proc_timestamp(tcp_ack ev, tcp_ctx ctx) {
    if (!(ctx.state != ST_CLOSED && ctx.state != ST_LISTEN && ctx.state != ST_SYN_RCVD))
        return;
    if (!ctx.saw_timestamp)
        ctx.ts_recent = ev.ts_val;
    return;
}

// §proc_window
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The peer's advertised window, RFC 793's rule.
//
// A SEPARATE PROCESSOR, AND AHEAD OF §proc_fast_retransmit, because the donor
// updates the window BEFORE it counts duplicates and the duplicate test asks
// whether that update moved the right edge. Folded into §proc_congestion, where
// it used to live, the test could not be written.
//
// The window moves only if this segment is newer by sequence, or is the same
// segment offering more (tcp_in.c:348-357) — snd_wl1/snd_wl2, kept so an old
// segment arriving late cannot shrink a window that has since opened. We
// assigned it unconditionally on every acknowledgement, so a reordered segment
// carrying a stale window shrank ours.
//
// Refreshed by EVERY acknowledgement, INCLUDING one that advances nothing: a
// pure window update carries no new acknowledgement number, and a window
// reopening IS an acknowledgement that advances nothing.
void proc_window(tcp_ack ev, tcp_ctx ctx) {
    uint32 cwindow;

    if (!(ctx.state != ST_CLOSED && ctx.state != ST_LISTEN && ctx.state != ST_SYN_RCVD))
        return;
    cwindow = uint32(ev.window) << ctx.snd_wscale;

    // Before the update, for the duplicate test that follows.
    ctx.right_wnd_edge = ctx.snd_wl2 + ctx.send_wnd;

    if (int32(ctx.snd_wl1 - ev.seq) < 0 ||
        (ctx.snd_wl1 == ev.seq && int32(ctx.snd_wl2 - ev.ack) < 0) ||
        (ctx.snd_wl2 == ev.ack && cwindow > ctx.send_wnd)) {
        ctx.send_wnd = cwindow;
        ctx.snd_wl1 = ev.seq;
        ctx.snd_wl2 = ev.ack;
    }
    return;
}

// §proc_rtt
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The round-trip estimate. Guarded on the acknowledgement ADVANCING, which is
// why it sits ahead of §proc_ack in the chain: §proc_ack is what moves
// send_una, so this must read it first.
void proc_rtt(tcp_ack ev, tcp_ctx ctx) {
    if (!(ctx.state != ST_CLOSED && ctx.state != ST_LISTEN && ctx.state != ST_SYN_RCVD))
        return;
    if (int32(ev.ack - ctx.send_una) <= 0)
        return;
    estimate_rtt(ctx, now(), ev.ts_ecr);
    return;
}

// §proc_fast_retransmit
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Retransmit on the third duplicate. D-30 settled what goes here and this does
// it: the donor's behaviour exactly, third duplicate only, INCLUDING its
// refusal to attempt a send on the fourth and later.
//
// THE DONOR'S FIVE CONDITIONS, in its own order (tcp_in.c:373-396): the
// acknowledgement is old, carries no payload, leaves the window's right edge
// where it was, and repeats the last one seen. `snd_wl2 + send_wnd` is the
// right edge AFTER §proc_window has run, so comparing it against the edge
// BEFORE is what "the window did not change" means.
void proc_fast_retransmit(tcp_ack ev, tcp_ctx ctx, tcp_scratch s) {
    bool dup = false;

    if (!(ctx.state != ST_CLOSED && ctx.state != ST_LISTEN && ctx.state != ST_SYN_RCVD))
        return;

    if (int32(ev.ack - ctx.send_next) < 0 && ev.ack == ctx.last_ack_seq &&
        ev.payload_len == 0 &&
        ctx.snd_wl2 + ctx.send_wnd == ctx.right_wnd_edge) {
        ctx.dup_acks = ctx.dup_acks + 1;
        dup = true;
    }
    if (!dup) {
        ctx.dup_acks = 0;
        ctx.last_ack_seq = ev.ack;
        return;
    }

    if (ctx.dup_acks == PARITY_DUP_ACK_THRESH) {
        // THE THIRD DUPLICATE, AND ONLY THE THIRD. Rewind to what the peer is
        // asking for and halve the window; §gen_seg is the next link but one
        // and does the attempt. This processor does not generate.
        if (int32(ev.ack - ctx.send_next) < 0)
            ctx.send_next = ev.ack;

        ctx.ssthresh = (ctx.cwnd < ctx.send_wnd ? ctx.cwnd : ctx.send_wnd) / 2;
        if (ctx.ssthresh < 2 * PARITY_MSS_ADVERTISED)
            ctx.ssthresh = 2 * PARITY_MSS_ADVERTISED;
        ctx.cwnd = ctx.ssthresh + 3 * PARITY_MSS_ADVERTISED;

        if (ctx.rtx_count < PARITY_MAX_RTX)
            ctx.rtx_count = ctx.rtx_count + 1;
    } else if (ctx.dup_acks > PARITY_DUP_ACK_THRESH) {
        // FOURTH AND LATER: INFLATE, AND ATTEMPT NOTHING. That is the donor
        // (tcp_in.c:466-473) and it is arguably wrong — NewReno inflates
        // precisely so the sender clocks out one segment per duplicate, and
        // mTCP inflates and never attempts, so it does no packet conservation
        // during recovery. D-30: match the donor, defect included, because
        // parity is measured against the donor and "more correct than the
        // donor" is a divergence like any other. REVISIT before any writeup — a
        // reader will expect NewReno here.
        if (ctx.cwnd + PARITY_MSS_ADVERTISED > ctx.cwnd)   // the C's overflow guard
            ctx.cwnd = ctx.cwnd + PARITY_MSS_ADVERTISED;
        s.no_send = true;
    } else {
        // first and second: counted, nothing else, no attempt
        s.no_send = true;
    }
    return;
}

// §proc_congestion
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The congestion window, and the guard for an acknowledgement past send_next.
void proc_congestion(tcp_ack ev, tcp_ctx ctx) {
    uint32 acked;

    if (!(ctx.state != ST_CLOSED && ctx.state != ST_LISTEN && ctx.state != ST_SYN_RCVD))
        return;

    // The peer's window is §proc_window's, ahead of this in the chain.
    acked = ev.ack - ctx.send_una;
    if (int32(acked) <= 0)
        return;

    // An acknowledgement PAST send_next, and BOTH halves of the donor's guard —
    // before send_una moves, which is the ordering that makes the comparison
    // meaningful. This is the NORMAL outcome of a successful recovery, not an
    // edge case: after a retransmission fills a hole the receiver has been
    // holding later data out of order, so its cumulative acknowledgement jumps
    // past everything we rewound send_next to.
    //
    // Both halves or neither. Taking `send_next = ack` alone would match the
    // donor's sequence numbers exactly while diverging on its congestion
    // trajectory — which is the observable rule 1 names, and which the packet
    // count and the size histogram would both pass.
    //
    // IT LIVES HERE, AHEAD OF THE GROWTH, and the pair must stay in this order:
    // `cwnd = ssthresh` is a reset that the growth below then builds on. Run the
    // growth first and a recovery would be credited twice.
    if (int32(ev.ack - ctx.send_next) > 0) {
        ctx.send_next = ev.ack;
        ctx.cwnd = ctx.ssthresh;
    }

    // Growth with the donor's granularity: packets is floor(acked / 1448), and
    // the `packets++` at tcp_in.c:519 is unreachable dead code — so AN
    // ACKNOWLEDGEMENT COVERING FEWER THAN 1448 NEW BYTES GROWS THE WINDOW BY
    // NOTHING AT ALL. Reproduced; "rounded up" was the earlier and wrong
    // reading.
    //
    // ssthresh is 0 on a passive open (D-01), so `cwnd < ssthresh` is false
    // from the first acknowledgement and this is always congestion avoidance.
    // The server has no slow start. Reproduce, do not correct.
    uint32 packets = acked / PARITY_MSS_PAYLOAD;

    if (packets > 0 && ctx.cwnd >= ctx.ssthresh)
        ctx.cwnd = ctx.cwnd + (PARITY_MSS_ADVERTISED * PARITY_MSS_ADVERTISED) / ctx.cwnd
                              * packets;
    else if (packets > 0)
        ctx.cwnd = ctx.cwnd + PARITY_MSS_ADVERTISED * packets;
    return;
}

// §proc_ack
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Retire the acknowledged bytes, and our own FIN, which consumes sequence
// space.
//
// It does not call the generator. §gen_seg follows it in every chain that can
// send, so the duplicate case that used to call the generator inline simply
// returns and the dispatch runs it — DEFERRED.md D2, one of the two places the
// dispatch was hand-inlined.
//
// EVERY PROCESSOR GUARDS ITSELF. The dispatch decides which chain runs, never
// which link of it does. `acked` is recomputed in each processor of this chain
// rather than threaded through the scratchpad, because this is the only writer
// of send_una and it runs last: all three read the same value.
void proc_ack(tcp_ack ev, tcp_ctx ctx, tcp_scratch s) {
    uint32 acked;

    if (!(ctx.state != ST_CLOSED && ctx.state != ST_LISTEN && ctx.state != ST_SYN_RCVD))
        return;

    // THE ACKNOWLEDGEMENT OF OUR OWN FIN, which consumes sequence space and so
    // is this processor's to retire. It sits AHEAD of the duplicate test
    // because a retransmitted acknowledgement of our FIN advances nothing and
    // must still complete the teardown.
    //
    // DESIGN-CLOSE.md §4's "peer FIN and ack of ours -> TIME_WAIT" row is
    // reached by two routes: a segment carrying both takes FIN_WAIT_1 ->
    // FIN_WAIT_2 here and then FIN_WAIT_2 -> TIME_WAIT in §proc_fin; the
    // CLOSING route is live for the ordering where the peer's FIN arrives first.
    if (ev.ack == ctx.send_next) {
        if (ctx.state == ST_LAST_ACK) {
            ctx.state = ST_CLOSED;
            del_ctx(ctx.key);
            // THE CONTEXT IS GONE. Every processor after this one in the chain
            // would run against freed memory, so the chain ends here. `del_ctx`
            // ends the chain it is issued from; nothing else in this program
            // ends a chain early.
            return;
        }
        if (ctx.state == ST_FIN_WAIT_1)
            ctx.state = ST_FIN_WAIT_2;
        else if (ctx.state == ST_CLOSING)
            ctx.state = ST_TIME_WAIT;
        timer_start(ctx.tw_timer, millisecond(PARITY_TIMEWAIT_MS));
    }

    acked = ev.ack - ctx.send_una;
    if (int32(acked) <= 0) {
        // ADVANCES NOTHING, BUT IT IS STILL AN EVENT. §proc_window has just
        // refreshed the window from this segment, and a peer whose window
        // reopens tells us with exactly this: ack == send_una, a larger window,
        // no new data acknowledged. §gen_seg, next in the chain, is what acts
        // on it (D-25).
        return;
    }
    s.acked = acked;

    if (ctx.fin_pending && int32(ev.ack - (ctx.fin_seq + 1)) >= 0)
        ctx.fin_pending = false;

    ctx.send_una = ev.ack;
    tx_flush_and_notify(ctx.tx, acked);

    // Progress: cancel, and reset the backoff. Re-armed if anything is still
    // outstanding.
    ctx.rtx_count = 0;
    timer_stop(ctx.rto_timer);
    if (unacked_on_wire(ctx))
timer_start(ctx.rto_timer,
                    millisecond((ctx.have_rtt ? ctx.rto_ms : PARITY_INITIAL_RTO_MS)
                                << (ctx.rtx_count < 7 ? ctx.rtx_count : 7)));
    return;
}

// §proc_recv
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Payload, in order or not: stores it at its own sequence number, marks the
// range in rx_wnd, slides, and recomputes the window if the boundary moved. The
// acknowledgement it owes is §send_ack's, next in the chain.
void proc_recv(tcp_data ev, tcp_ctx ctx, tcp_scratch s) {
    uint32 next;

    // The data-acknowledgement path, silenced permanently once a FIN has been
    // accepted (D-20). ONE STATE TEST THAT SAYS WHAT IT MEANS, which is the
    // prototype's mechanism rather than mTCP's emergent counter arithmetic —
    // reproducing that would mean reproducing the arithmetic to obtain a side
    // effect.
    if (!(ctx.state == ST_ESTABLISHED || ctx.state == ST_FIN_WAIT_1 || ctx.state == ST_FIN_WAIT_2))
        return;
    if (ev.data_len == 0)
        return;

    if (!ctx.rx_open) {
        new_rx_ordered_data(ctx.rx, INF);
        ctx.rx_open = true;
    }

    // Store the bytes at their OWN sequence number. The target copies to an
    // offset; it does not interpret and reports nothing back. A refusal here is
    // the window being full, not the segment being out of order.
    if (add_rx_data_seg(ctx.rx, ev.hold_addr, ev.data_len, ev.seq) < 0)
        return;

    // Mark the range as arrived, wherever it landed, then advance over
    // everything contiguously arrived. THERE IS NO IN-ORDER BRANCH BECAUSE
    // THERE DOES NOT NEED TO BE ONE: a segment past the boundary marks a range
    // the slide does not reach, and a later segment that fills the gap lets the
    // same slide run past both. Duplicates, overlaps and multiple holes need no
    // code at all — marking a range twice is the same as marking it once, a
    // partial overlap marks the union, and two losses leave two runs with the
    // slide stopping at the first.
    sw_set(ctx.rx_wnd, ev.seq, ev.seq + ev.data_len);
    next = sw_slide(ctx.rx_wnd);

    if (next != ctx.recv_next) {
        // §window_rule RECOMPUTE POINT 1. Nothing else in this program writes
        // rcv_wnd.
        ctx.recv_next = next;
        ctx.rcv_wnd = PARITY_RCVBUF_SIZE - ((ctx.recv_next - ctx.delivered)
                      - ((ctx.fin_consumed && ctx.recv_next != ctx.delivered) ? 1 : 0));
        s.ack_opt = ACK_AGGREGATE;
    } else {
        // THE DONOR'S DISCRIMINATOR, and the reason ack_cnt is a count.
        // ProcessTCPPayload returns FALSE exactly when the contiguous boundary
        // did not advance, and Handle_TCP_ST_ESTABLISHED answers that with
        // ACK_OPT_NOW rather than ACK_OPT_AGGREGATE.
        //
        // So each segment landing past a hole owes its OWN duplicate
        // acknowledgement. That is the signal that trips the peer's fast
        // retransmit, and it only works if the duplicates are counted rather
        // than merged: three segments past the hole must produce three.
        //
        // Stored past a gap the boundary did not move, so nothing new is
        // acknowledged and the window rule has nothing to recompute. THE
        // ACKNOWLEDGEMENT STILL GOES OUT, repeating the same cumulative number.
        s.ack_opt = ACK_APIECE;
    }
    return;
}

// §owe_ack
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE DONOR'S EnqueueACK, and nothing else (tcp_out.c:1088-1096). How often to
// acknowledge is a protocol decision, so it is not ours to tune.
//
// A byte threshold used to sit here, chosen by sweeping throughput. That was
// the wrong kind of answer to the wrong question — it made the number of
// acknowledgements a performance parameter, when it is part of what the two
// stacks have to agree on before any performance number means anything.
void owe_ack(tcp_ctx ctx, uint8 opt) {
    if (opt == ACK_NONE)
        return;
    if (opt == ACK_AGGREGATE) {
        if (ctx.ack_cnt == 0)
            ctx.ack_cnt = 1;
    } else {
        ctx.ack_cnt = ctx.ack_cnt + 1;
    }
    // D3: ASK TO BE ATTEMPTED AGAIN NEXT PASS. The emission is §drain_owed_acks
    // and it runs on the generate half, so what is owed here is emitted there.
    retry(ctx);
    return;
}

// §send_ack
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Acknowledgement generation — record what the chain decided it owed.
//
// IT RE-TESTS NOTHING. Every guard that decides whether an acknowledgement is
// due — the receive-side state test, the in-order test, the FIN's ordering — lives at the
// decision site, and this only acts on `s.ack_opt`. That is what makes it safe
// to run AFTER §proc_fin's state transition: the old code had to build the
// FIN's acknowledgement BEFORE the transition, because the guard that silences
// the data path would otherwise have silenced it too. With the decision
// separated from the emission, the ordering hazard is gone rather than
// commented.
//
//   THE POST-FIN SUPPRESSION IS PER-PATH, NOT PER-CONNECTION (D-20, corrected).
//
//   The invariant: once a FIN is accepted, the DATA-acknowledgement path stops
//   producing acknowledgements for that stream, permanently. The CONTROL path
//   does not.
//
//   Pure acknowledgements after a FIN are REQUIRED and the donor sends them,
//   from the control path: CLOSE_WAIT at tcp_out.c:632-634 ("Send ACK for the
//   FIN here"), FIN_WAIT_2 at :655-657, TIME_WAIT at :675-677. The CLOSE_WAIT
//   one is what acknowledges the peer's FIN. Suppress it and teardown stalls
//   and the peer retransmits its FIN.
//
//   What stops is only the data path. In the donor the sequence test at
//   tcp_out.c:875-879 can only fail once a FIN has advanced rcv_nxt past the
//   merged data, and that sum is invariant under application reads, so it holds
//   for a connection's whole life and then fails permanently from the moment a
//   FIN is accepted. It is de-duplication within that path, not silence.
//
//   REPRODUCE THE EFFECT, NOT THE STRUCTURE. "The control walk got there first"
//   is not a mechanism available to us — we have one queue, not three — so what
//   has to hold is the observable, and the observable is per-path.
//
//   An earlier version of this note said "one fewer pure acknowledgement per
//   teardown", from a wrong reading of D-20. Built to that, teardown would have
//   stalled and looked like a teardown bug rather than a wrong premise. Left
//   visible because that is the failure the charter warns about.
//
//   USE THE PROTOTYPE'S MECHANISM, NOT THE DONOR'S. Both reach the same economy
//   — one acknowledgement per teardown carrying the FIN-inclusive ack_seq, no
//   duplicate — but by different means. mTCP's suppression is EMERGENT: ack_cnt
//   is dropped because rcv_nxt has moved past head_seq + merged_len and nothing
//   restores it, so reproducing it faithfully would mean reproducing mTCP's
//   counter arithmetic to obtain a side effect. The prototype has an explicit
//   state guard instead — an unconditional early return once the context is in
//   CLOSE_WAIT (mtp_ep.c:1028-1033), with the same guard on the data path at
//   :959. One state test, it says what it means, and it is checkable by reading.
//
//   And do NOT copy the donor's state lists. EnqueueACK's expected set
//   (tcp_out.c:1082-1086) is four states; to_ack's is those four PLUS
//   TIME_WAIT. Neither is a superset of the other's intent, so at least one is
//   an oversight. Implement the per-path rule, not either list.
void send_ack(event_t ev, tcp_ctx ctx, tcp_scratch s) {
    return owe_ack(ctx, s.ack_opt);
}


// §proc_fin
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Teardown: the peer closes.
//
// Ordering, from the prototype and easy to invert: the acknowledgement is
// DECIDED before the state transition, so it is still emitted for the FIN
// itself. Transition first and the guard that silences the data path would
// silence this too. Out of order is covered as well — a FIN arriving ahead of a
// gap does not transition, and the later segment that fills the hole makes this
// processor perform the transition and still owe the acknowledgement, because
// the guard was evaluated before it. Either route owes exactly once.
void proc_fin(tcp_fin ev, tcp_ctx ctx, tcp_scratch s) {
    uint32 fin_seq;

    if (!(ctx.state == ST_ESTABLISHED || ctx.state == ST_FIN_WAIT_1 || ctx.state == ST_FIN_WAIT_2))
        return;

    // THE FIN'S SEQUENCE IS PAST ITS OWN PAYLOAD, which is the donor's test
    // verbatim: `if (seq + payloadlen == cur_stream->rcv_nxt)`, under its own
    // comment "FIN packet is allowed to push payload". Testing ev.seq alone
    // refuses a data-carrying FIN as out of order and the connection never
    // tears down. §proc_recv has already run for this segment — the parser
    // raises tcp_data before tcp_fin — so recv_next is past the payload by now
    // if the payload was in order.
    fin_seq = ev.seq + ev.payload_len;
    if (fin_seq != ctx.recv_next) {
        // A FIN ahead of a gap does not transition; the segment that fills the
        // hole performs it. It is still ACKNOWLEDGED, which is the donor's else
        // branch — without it the peer learns nothing and retransmits blind.
        s.ack_opt = ACK_APIECE;
        return;
    }

    ctx.recv_next = fin_seq + 1;        // the FIN consumes one byte
    sw_set(ctx.rx_wnd, fin_seq, fin_seq + 1);
    sw_slide(ctx.rx_wnd);      // keep the window's boundary and
                                        // recv_next the same number
    ctx.fin_consumed = true;            // ...which is NOT data: G14

    s.ack_opt = ACK_APIECE;             // §send_ack records it, after the
                                        // transition below

    // DESIGN-CLOSE.md §4. Where the peer's FIN takes us depends on whether we
    // have already sent ours; CLOSING is the simultaneous close, one row and
    // worth it — leaving it out wedges exactly the way the missing active close
    // did.
    if (ctx.state == ST_FIN_WAIT_1) {
        ctx.state = ST_CLOSING;
    } else if (ctx.state == ST_FIN_WAIT_2) {
        ctx.state = ST_TIME_WAIT;
        timer_start(ctx.tw_timer, millisecond(PARITY_TIMEWAIT_MS));
    } else {
        bool was_established = ctx.state == ST_ESTABLISHED;

        ctx.state = ST_CLOSE_WAIT;
        // D8: READABLE, AND ONLY OUT OF ESTABLISHED. The donor raises a read
        // event here and nowhere else on the FIN path (tcp_in.c:954,
        // immediately after the CLOSE_WAIT transition and inside the branch
        // only ESTABLISHED reaches). It is READABLE because that is what an
        // application waiting on a socket acts on: read() returning 0 is how
        // end-of-stream is delivered, and a state notification is not something
        // the donor's API has. The simultaneous-close and FIN_WAIT_2 rows above
        // raise nothing, which is also the donor.
        if (was_established)
            notify(ctx, READABLE);
    }
    return;
}

// §gen_fin
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Our FIN, gated on send_next == write_end (G10).
//
// The constraint is narrower than "order the FIN". Against data already turned
// into blueprints the per-flow ring is FIFO, so a FIN committed after them
// cannot overtake them. What must not happen is a FIN ahead of data the program
// has NOT yet handed to the target — the window- or congestion-limited case —
// which in the prototype goes out at one past ALL buffered data, leaving a
// sequence gap and a second FIN once the buffer drains. So the gate is exactly:
// send_next has caught up with write_end. The prototype gates this in one of
// its two close paths and not the other, which is the evidence it is an
// oversight rather than a decision.
//
// DESIGN-CLOSE.md §3: the condition is that the APPLICATION has closed and
// everything it handed us has been sent. Whether we then go to LAST_ACK or
// FIN_WAIT_1 is a CONSEQUENCE of whether the peer closed first, not a
// precondition for closing at all. The old gate was `state == CLOSE_WAIT`,
// which encoded "the peer closed first" as a requirement — and against a peer
// that never closes first, which is any mTCP peer and so every peer we will
// ever measure against, the object was delivered and the connection then hung
// for ever with no FIN.
void gen_fin(event_t ev, tcp_ctx ctx) {
    if (!ctx.app_closed)
        return;
    if (ctx.state != ST_ESTABLISHED && ctx.state != ST_CLOSE_WAIT)
        return;                     // already closing, or not yet open
    if (ctx.send_next - ctx.snd_base != ctx.write_end)
        return;                     // G10: not ahead of unsent data

    if (pkt_gen(ctx, build_hdr(ctx, ctx.send_next, FLAG_ACK | FLAG_FIN, now()), PRIO_DATA, false) == 0) {
        ctx.fin_pending = true;
        ctx.fin_seq = ctx.send_next;
        ctx.send_next = ctx.send_next + 1;      // the FIN consumes one byte
        ctx.state = ctx.state == ST_CLOSE_WAIT ? ST_LAST_ACK : ST_FIN_WAIT_1;
    }
    return;
}


// §mark_closed
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The application has no more to send.
//
// Split out of the close handler so that `app_close -> { mark_closed, gen_fin }`
// is a chain the dispatch runs, not a processor calling a generator. That
// direct call was the second of DEFERRED.md D2's two hand-inlined dispatches;
// the first was §proc_ack's call to the generator.
void mark_closed(app_close ev, tcp_ctx ctx) {
    ctx.app_closed = true;
    return;
}

// §record_data
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Application data appended to the transmit stream.
//
// RUNS ON THE CALLING THREAD, which is what makes the instruction below the
// thing that CAUSES the copy rather than a note about one that already
// happened. It touches the stream and `write_end`; neither generates a packet,
// so nothing here belongs to the stack. The count it returns is what the
// application is told it wrote, and a short return is back-pressure, not an
// error.
//
// `write_end` is the program's own mirror of how much it has appended — the
// target has no accessor to ask, and the withdrawal of the target's read side
// rests on exactly this working.
void record_data(app_send ev, tcp_ctx ctx) {
    uint32 wrote;

    if (!(ctx.state == ST_ESTABLISHED || ctx.state == ST_CLOSE_WAIT) || !ctx.tx_open) {
        ev.result = -1;
        return;
    }

    wrote = add_tx_data(ctx.tx, ev.addr, ev.mlen);
    if (wrote > 0)
        ctx.write_end = ctx.write_end + wrote;
    // What the application is told it wrote. A short return is back-pressure,
    // not an error.
    ev.result = int32(wrote);
    return;
}

// §gen_seg
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The send decision, under min(cwnd, peer window).
//
// ONE pkt_gen FOR THE WHOLE SENDABLE RUN; the target segments it and the
// segmentation rule assigns each segment's sequence number. That is deferred
// segmentation, and it is why the recurrence has to be per BYTE: the program
// does not know, and must not need to know, how the run was split.
//
// THE DONOR'S LOOP PREDICATE, not a restatement of it:
//
//     remaining_window = MIN(cwnd, peer_wnd) - (seq - snd_una)
//     if (remaining_window <= 0 ||
//         (remaining_window < mss && seq - snd_una > 0)) bail
//     len     = MIN(len, remaining_window)
//     pkt_len = MIN(len, mss - 12)
//
// Two things a rounding rule would lose, and both matter:
//
//   `seq - snd_una > 0` IS AN ESCAPE. With nothing in flight the rule does not
//   apply and the donor sends whatever fits, down to one byte. Rounding down to
//   a multiple of the payload maximum with no escape withholds the last partial
//   segment for ever whenever the buffer does not refill — which is the end of
//   every object. Nine short segments traded for a transfer that never
//   completes.
//
//   The predicate is on unused WINDOW, not on the amount available to send:
//   `len` does not appear in it. The two coincide in the common case and come
//   apart when the window binds rather than the data.
//
// G2, the sender-side silly-window rule, is the DONOR'S and not the prototype's
// rounding: refuse a sub-MSS segment when anything is in flight. And it is
// reproduced WITH THE DONOR'S CONSTANTS — the window is compared against 1460
// while a segment consumes 1448. That mismatch is NOT why an RTO sends one
// segment: after an RTO cwnd is 1460, the first segment consumes 1448, and the
// remaining 12 is below either threshold, so the bail fires identically
// whichever constant is used. One segment per RTO follows from cwnd == MSS
// while a segment consumes MSS - 12, and nothing else. The mismatch is
// observable only when min(cwnd, peer_wnd) - inflight lands in [1448, 1460).
//
// THE LOOP IS BOUNDED by min(cwnd, send_wnd) / PARITY_MSS_PAYLOAD: every iteration
// either advances `seq` by a positive amount or breaks.
//
// IT RETURNS THE NUMBER OF PACKETS IT PUT ON THE WIRE, which is the donor's
// SendTCPPacket return value and what its caller subtracts from `ack_cnt`:
// every data packet carries the ACK flag, so a packet sent is an
// acknowledgement that no longer needs its own frame. One pkt_gen can become
// several segments, so the count is derived from the extent handed over rather
// than from the number of calls.
//
// NOTE ON `inflight`. It counts GENERATED-unacknowledged, which under deferred
// segmentation includes blueprints still in the ring. Basing it on the EMITTED
// position instead — which is what "in flight" ought to mean — removed the only
// thing bounding GENERATION: with a backlog, emitted lags generated, in flight
// reads low, the loop generates further ahead, the ring fills and the flow
// stalls. Measured: 0, 0 and 4 completions in three 60-second runs at 128 MB
// against 28, 26 and 28 before the change. The congestion window should bound
// what is on the wire while something else bounds how far generation may run
// ahead of it, and there is no second bound — the conflation currently supplies
// it. Splitting them is a design change (docs/DESIGN.md §25), not a
// substitution.
void gen_seg(event_t ev, tcp_ctx ctx, tcp_scratch s) {
    uint32 win, to_send, seq, pkts;   // `pkts`: see s.pkts_sent
    bool   window_blocked = false;
    bool   held_back = false;
    bool   rtx;

    // THE DONOR DOES NOT ATTEMPT A SEND on a duplicate it is not acting on
    // (D-30). We attempted on every one — the D-25 stall fix — and the
    // per-iteration retry list is what took that recovery over.
    if (s.no_send)
        return;
    if (!(ctx.state == ST_ESTABLISHED || ctx.state == ST_CLOSE_WAIT))
        return;

    win = ctx.cwnd < ctx.send_wnd ? ctx.cwnd : ctx.send_wnd;
    seq = ctx.send_next;
    to_send = 0;

    while (true) {
        uint32 inflight_here = seq - ctx.send_una;
        int32  rw = int32(win) - int32(inflight_here);
        uint32 avail_here, len, pkt;

        if (rw <= 0) {
            window_blocked = true;
            break;
        }
        if (rw < int32(PARITY_MSS_ADVERTISED) && inflight_here > 0) {
            held_back = true;           // G2, the silly-window hold-off
            break;
        }
        avail_here = ctx.write_end - (seq - ctx.snd_base);
        if (avail_here == 0)
            break;                      // nothing buffered

        len = avail_here < uint32(rw) ? avail_here : uint32(rw);
        pkt = len < PARITY_MSS_PAYLOAD ? len : PARITY_MSS_PAYLOAD;
        to_send = to_send + pkt;
        seq = seq + pkt;
    }

    if (to_send == 0) {
        // D3: ASK TO BE ATTEMPTED AGAIN NEXT PASS. The donor keeps a
        // window-blocked stream on its send list and retries every pass; we
        // retried on EVENTS, which coincides with it whenever the window
        // reopens BECAUSE an acknowledgement arrived, and differs for
        // back-pressure that resolves without an inbound packet — ring space
        // freed by our own drain, most of all.
        //
        // Asked for on every refusal that has something to send, not only the
        // window one: the hold-off is also "held back, will go later", while
        // "nothing buffered" and "wrong state" have nothing to retry.
        if (window_blocked || held_back)
            retry(ctx);
        // AND THE PROBE AS WELL, not instead. The retry recovers a window whose
        // reopening is announced; this recovers one whose announcement was LOST,
        // and no retry substitutes for a message that never arrives (D-25
        // piece 2).
        if (window_blocked)
            timer_start(ctx.probe_timer, millisecond(PARITY_PROBE_MS));
        return;
    }

    // Below the high-water mark is a RETRANSMISSION, whoever asked. Marking the
    // CALLER did not work: the timeout rewinds and its own send is refused, so
    // the re-emission happens later from the acknowledgement path — the rewind
    // and the re-send are separated in time.
    rtx = ctx.send_next < ctx.send_high;

    TCPBP bp = build_hdr(ctx, ctx.send_next, FLAG_ACK, now());

    // The segmentation rule is the fourth argument, written at the call. TCP's
    // recurrence is per BYTE: the next segment's sequence number is the previous
    // one's plus the previous payload length.
    bp.data = unseg_data(ctx.tx, ctx.send_next - ctx.snd_base, to_send,
                         PARITY_MSS_PAYLOAD,
                         [TCPBP::seq_no, ctx.send_next,
                          prev.seq_no + prev.payload_len]);

    if (pkt_gen(ctx, bp, PRIO_DATA, rtx) != 0)
        return;

    // What the target will cut this extent into, which is what the donor's
    // SendTCPPacket would have returned.
    pkts = (to_send + PARITY_MSS_PAYLOAD - 1) / PARITY_MSS_PAYLOAD;
    s.pkts_sent = s.pkts_sent + pkts;
    ctx.send_next = ctx.send_next + to_send;
    if (ctx.send_next > ctx.send_high)
        ctx.send_high = ctx.send_next;
    if (unacked_on_wire(ctx))
timer_start(ctx.rto_timer,
                    millisecond((ctx.have_rtt ? ctx.rto_ms : PARITY_INITIAL_RTO_MS)
                                << (ctx.rtx_count < 7 ? ctx.rtx_count : 7)));
    return;
}

// §drain_owed_acks
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DATA FIRST, AND THAT ORDER IS THE POINT. The donor drains its send list
// before its acknowledgement list, and every data packet it sends carries the
// ACK flag — so it subtracts the packets sent from `ack_cnt` and only sends
// separately whatever is still owed (ACK_PIGGYBACK, tcp_out.c:806-817, TRUE in
// the donor's build).
//
// We had it backwards: acknowledgements first, then data. Every acknowledgement
// therefore went out as its own packet even when a data packet in the same pass
// was about to carry one. Invisible on a client, which sends nothing to carry
// them; on a server it is a pure acknowledgement for every segment that a data
// segment already acknowledged.
//
// THEN WHAT IS STILL OWED, in a loop. The donor's WriteTCPACKList sends them
// one at a time and stops early only when the transmit buffer refuses one
// (tcp_out.c:894-903); this stops on the same condition, and what is left stays
// owed for the next pass rather than being dropped. Bounded by `ack_cnt`, which
// only §owe_ack increases and only this decreases.
void drain_owed_acks(event_t ev, tcp_ctx ctx, tcp_scratch s) {
    uint32 sent = s.pkts_sent;


    ctx.ack_cnt = ctx.ack_cnt > sent ? ctx.ack_cnt - sent : 0;

    while (ctx.ack_cnt > 0) {
        // This carries the window too, so the advertisement is discharged with
        // it.
        ctx.need_wnd_adv = false;
        if (pkt_gen(ctx, build_hdr(ctx, ctx.send_next, FLAG_ACK, now()),
                    PRIO_ACK, false) != 0)
            break;                      // no room; still owed
        ctx.ack_cnt = ctx.ack_cnt - 1;
    }
    return;
}

// §gen_wnd_adv
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// D11, FIRE. The application drained and the window reopened; the peer is still
// holding the zero we last advertised and will not send again until something
// tells it otherwise. The donor enqueues an acknowledgement on its ackq from
// CopyToUser and its stack sends it (api.c:1144-1151), gated on the window now
// exceeding one segment.
//
// HERE RATHER THAN IN §proc_drain because that runs on the APPLICATION thread
// and generation is the stack's (CR-4). §proc_drain raises the flag and asks
// for a retry; this is the retry.
//
// It is fatal for a CLIENT and invisible for a server. A server mostly sends,
// so it has an acknowledgement to carry the new window on anyway; a client
// mostly receives, and without this its window reopens in its own state and
// nowhere else. Measured before the fix: data arrived in 86 bursts over 15
// seconds, one per peer window probe.
//
// LAST, which is also where the donor puts it: is_wack is sent after the
// ack_cnt loop in the same walk.
// The generate half's answer to the application: the extent it was handed,
// which the application has already buffered (CR-4). A closed send path is a
// refusal.
void gen_answer(app_gen ev, tcp_ctx ctx) {
    ev.result = (ctx.state == ST_ESTABLISHED || ctx.state == ST_CLOSE_WAIT) ? int32(ev.mlen) : -1;
    return;
}

void gen_wnd_adv(event_t ev, tcp_ctx ctx) {
    if (!ctx.need_wnd_adv || ctx.rcv_wnd <= PARITY_MSS_PAYLOAD)
        return;
    ctx.need_wnd_adv = false;
    pkt_gen(ctx, build_hdr(ctx, ctx.send_next, FLAG_ACK, now()),
                    PRIO_ACK, false);
    return;
}

// §proc_drain
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The application took bytes: §window_rule's RECOMPUTE POINT 2. `delivered`
// advances by what the instruction REPORTS it handed over, so the accounting
// cannot drift from what the target actually gave the application.
//
// The window that results is advertised on the next segment this flow sends —
// one main-loop iteration later, as the donor does it. mTCP's application
// thread never emits a packet, so deferring by one iteration is the donor's
// behaviour and not a shortcut.
//
// EMPTY IS NOT THE SAME AS ENDED, and the application interface has to be able
// to tell them apart. The donor's read returns 0 only at end-of-stream — the
// peer has closed and the buffer is drained — and EAGAIN when the buffer merely
// happens to be empty. We returned 0 for both, and epwget recognises a
// completed download ONLY in its `rd == 0` branch, so a client on our stack
// received every byte of every object and never once counted a completion.
void proc_drain(app_recv ev, tcp_ctx ctx, tcp_scratch s) {
    s.delivered = rx_flush_and_notify(ctx.rx, ev.len, ev.buf);
    if (s.delivered == 0) {
        // EMPTY IS NOT THE SAME AS ENDED. The donor's read returns 0 only at
        // end-of-stream -- the peer has closed and the buffer is drained -- and
        // -1 when the buffer merely happens to be empty. We returned 0 for
        // both, and epwget recognises a completed download ONLY in its `rd == 0`
        // branch, so a client received every byte of every object and never
        // counted a completion.
        ev.result = ctx.fin_consumed ? 0 : -1;
        return;
    }
    ev.result = int32(s.delivered);
    sock_recv(ctx, s.delivered);        // §window_rule RECOMPUTE POINT 2
    return;
}

// §proc_rst
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The peer reset the connection. Reproduced from the donor's ProcessRST, and
// TWO OF ITS ODDITIES ARE DELIBERATE:
//
//   - a reset on an ESTABLISHED connection leaves it in CLOSE_WAIT, not CLOSED,
//     and destroys nothing. The donor's own destroy is commented out there. So
//     the application still has to close it and the flow stays alive until it
//     does;
//   - the application is not told WHY. NotifyConnectionReset is commented out
//     in the donor; it raises a close event, so the application learns the
//     socket is readable and then reads end-of-stream.
//
// The donor also has `TODO: we need reset validation logic` at the top and does
// NOT check that the reset's sequence lies in the window — the check RFC 5961
// exists for. Reproducing that reproduces its exposure to blind reset attacks,
// which is what rule 1 asks for and is worth saying out loud. REVISIT before
// any writeup.
//
// A reset is never acknowledged and never answered with another reset.
void proc_rst(tcp_rst ev, tcp_ctx ctx) {
    // Below ESTABLISHED the donor does not handle it here at all.
    if (ctx.state == ST_CLOSED || ctx.state == ST_LISTEN)
        return;

    if (ctx.state == ST_SYN_RCVD) {
        // Only a reset that acknowledges our SYN-ACK exactly.
        if (ev.ack == ctx.send_next) {
            ctx.state = ST_CLOSED;
            del_ctx(ctx.key);
        }
        return;
    }

    if (ctx.state == ST_FIN_WAIT_1 || ctx.state == ST_FIN_WAIT_2 ||
        ctx.state == ST_LAST_ACK || ctx.state == ST_CLOSING ||
        ctx.state == ST_TIME_WAIT) {
        // Already closing; the reset just ends it.
        ctx.state = ST_CLOSED;
        del_ctx(ctx.key);
        return;
    }

    // ESTABLISHED or CLOSE_WAIT: see the note above.
    ctx.state = ST_CLOSE_WAIT;
    ctx.fin_consumed = true;            // the receive side is over, however it
                                        // ended: nothing more will arrive
    notify(ctx, READABLE);
    return;
}

// §gen_syn
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Our SYN, emitted or re-attempted.
//
// The active open's first packet. It is a GENERATION step, not part of the open
// chain, so the same retry that re-attempts a blocked send re-attempts an
// unsent SYN — one path, not two.
//
// A REFUSED SYN IS NORMAL AND IS NOT A FAILED CONNECT. The first packet to an
// unresolved peer ALWAYS fails: the target sends an address request and returns
// "retry later". Destroying the context there meant every connect to a peer we
// had not spoken to died, and since the client speaks first, that was every
// connect — the server never saw a SYN.
void gen_syn(event_t ev, tcp_ctx ctx) {
    if (ctx.state != ST_SYN_SENT || ctx.send_next != ctx.snd_base)
        return;                     // already on the wire

    if (pkt_gen(ctx, build_hdr(ctx, ctx.send_next, FLAG_SYN, now()),
                PRIO_CONTROL, false) != 0) {
        retry(ctx);          // the address is not resolved yet
        return;
    }
    ctx.send_next = ctx.send_next + 1;  // the SYN consumes one
    // ...SO DATA STARTS ONE PAST, and snd_base is what says where. §gen_seg
    // reads the payload at `send_next - snd_base`, so a snd_base that does not
    // account for the SYN sends from offset one — skipping the first byte of
    // the request and running one byte past its end. The passive open sets it
    // on the same line after its SYN-ACK; the active open set it BEFORE the SYN
    // and never moved it. The symptom was a request the peer never
    // acknowledged and never answered, on both servers, with everything up to
    // that point working.
    ctx.snd_base = ctx.send_next;
timer_start(ctx.rto_timer,
                millisecond((ctx.have_rtt ? ctx.rto_ms : PARITY_INITIAL_RTO_MS)
                            << (ctx.rtx_count < 7 ? ctx.rtx_count : 7)));
    return;
}

// §proc_synack
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The peer accepted our connection.
//
// Seeds everything the passive open seeds from a SYN, from the mirror image:
// the peer's initial sequence number, its window and scale, its timestamp, and
// whether it timestamps at all.
//
// THREE THINGS THE DESIGN DOCUMENTS DID NOT HAVE, found by reading the donor's
// HandleActiveOpen and the SYN_SENT arm of Handle_TCP_ST_SYN_SENT:
//
//   - mtcp_connect sets cwnd = 1 and this does `cwnd = (cwnd == 1) ? mss *
//     TCP_INIT_CWND : mss`, so the initial window arrives HERE and not at
//     connect;
//   - the ACTIVE open sets ssthresh = mss * 10, where the passive open never
//     assigns it at all. D-01 is that asymmetry seen from the other side, and
//     it means an active-open connection DOES slow-start where our server does
//     not;
//   - completion reaches the application as a WRITABLE event, not a state
//     event. That is what makes a blocked connect() return.
//
// THE TEST THAT THE ACKNOWLEDGEMENT IS OURS IS AGAINST send_next, not against
// snd_base. The donor's test is `ack_seq <= iss || ack_seq > snd_nxt` — above
// the INITIAL sequence number and no higher than what we have sent — and in
// SYN_SENT exactly one byte of sequence space is outstanding, so that range has
// exactly one member. Written against `snd_base`, which is ISS + 1 once the SYN
// has gone out, `ack <= snd_base` was true for the CORRECT acknowledgement and
// every handshake was answered with a reset. It only appeared when snd_base was
// corrected to account for the SYN; before that the two bugs cancelled.
void proc_synack(tcp_synack ev, tcp_ctx ctx) {
    if (ctx.state != ST_SYN_SENT)
        return;

    if (ev.ack != ctx.send_next) {
        gen_rst_from_ctx(ctx, ev.ack);
        return;
    }

    ctx.send_una  = ev.ack;
    ctx.recv_next = ev.seq + 1;         // irs + 1
    ctx.rcv_base  = ctx.recv_next;
    // `delivered` IS NOT SEEDED HERE, and the passive open seeds it. That
    // asymmetry is in the emitted C as it stands (prog_tcp.c §proc_synack
    // against §proc_passive_open) and this program records it rather than
    // repairing it, because the program is the authority for what the target
    // does and a silent repair would make the two disagree.
    //
    // BY THE ARGUMENT IN §proc_passive_open IT IS A DEFECT: §window_rule
    // computes PARITY_RCVBUF_SIZE - (recv_next - delivered), recv_next is seeded from the
    // peer's PARITY_ISN, and `delivered` at zero makes that difference a sequence
    // number rather than a byte count. It is invisible today only because the
    // PARITY_ISN is frozen at 0 (D-01's second freeze), which makes the two origins
    // coincide -- the same masking D-01 records for snd_wl1, and it lifts the
    // moment the PARITY_ISN does. FOR THE LEAD: fix in the C first, then here.
    sw_init(ctx.rx_wnd, ctx.recv_next);
    ctx.snd_wl1 = ev.seq - 1;
    ctx.snd_wl2 = ev.ack;
    ctx.last_ack_seq = ev.ack;
    ctx.saw_timestamp = ev.has_ts;
    ctx.ts_recent = ev.ts_val;
    if (ev.has_wscale)
        ctx.snd_wscale = ev.wscale;
    ctx.send_wnd = uint32(ev.window) << ctx.snd_wscale;

    ctx.cwnd = PARITY_INIT_CWND;               // mss * TCP_INIT_CWND
    ctx.ssthresh = PARITY_SSTHRESH_ACTIVE;     // the passive open sets none
    ctx.rtx_count = 0;
    timer_stop(ctx.rto_timer);

    if (!ctx.tx_open) {
        new_tx_ordered_data(ctx.tx, INF);
        ctx.tx_open = true;
    }
    ctx.state = ST_ESTABLISHED;

    notify(ctx, WRITABLE);

    // The handshake's own acknowledgement.
    pkt_gen(ctx, build_hdr(ctx, ctx.send_next, FLAG_ACK, now()),
                    PRIO_ACK, false);
    return;
}

// §proc_rto
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The retransmission timeout, with backoff.
//
// After an RTO the donor sets cwnd = MSS and ssthresh = max(cwnd/2, 2*MSS). One
// segment leaves, and the reason is that cwnd == MSS while a segment consumes
// MSS - 12, so §gen_seg's window bail fires after the first segment regardless
// of which constant it is compared against. It is NOT the 1460-versus-1448
// mismatch, which was the earlier and wrong explanation.
//
// G11: closed at sixteen attempts, and the application is told with an ERROR.
void proc_rto(tcp_rto_timeout ev, tcp_ctx ctx, tcp_scratch s) {
    ctx.rtx_count = ctx.rtx_count + 1;
    if (ctx.rtx_count > PARITY_MAX_RTX) {
        ctx.state = ST_CLOSED;
        notify(ctx, ERROR);
        return;
    }

    ctx.ssthresh = ctx.cwnd / 2 > 2 * PARITY_MSS_ADVERTISED ? ctx.cwnd / 2
                                                     : 2 * PARITY_MSS_ADVERTISED;
    ctx.cwnd = PARITY_MSS_ADVERTISED;

    // Retransmit from the OLDEST UNACKNOWLEDGED byte, not the unsent tail
    // (G13 — the prototype sizes this from the wrong end).
    ctx.send_next = ctx.send_una;
    gen_seg(ev, ctx, s);

    // Resend the FIN if it is what is outstanding. §gen_seg above emits payload
    // only, and §gen_fin refuses in LAST_ACK and FIN_WAIT_1, so without this the
    // rewind puts send_next back to the FIN's sequence and nothing ever
    // reoccupies it — the flow waits for an acknowledgement of a FIN that is no
    // longer on the wire.
    if (ctx.fin_pending && ctx.send_next == ctx.fin_seq &&
        pkt_gen(ctx, build_hdr(ctx, ctx.send_next, FLAG_ACK | FLAG_FIN, now()), PRIO_DATA, true) == 0)
        ctx.send_next = ctx.send_next + 1;
    return;
}

// §proc_timewait_done
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The TIME_WAIT interval expired; the context goes.
//
// D-24 reproduces the DONOR'S GUARD, not its timer value: even at
// tcp_timewait = 0 the state must be entered and left, because skipping it does
// not skip a wait, it removes the place the final acknowledgement is owed from.
// That acknowledgement was committed to the target in the iteration that
// entered this state, so by the time this fires it has drained.
//
// NOTHING RETRANSMITS THAT ACKNOWLEDGEMENT, deliberately: the donor does not.
// If it is lost the stream is already gone, the peer's retransmitted FIN finds
// no flow-table entry and is answered with a bare reset (§gen_rst), and
// teardown completes on that instead. Wire-observable when it happens, and the
// donor's behaviour rather than an omission.
void proc_timewait_done(tcp_timewait_timeout ev, tcp_ctx ctx) {
    ctx.state = ST_CLOSED;
    del_ctx(ctx.key);
    return;
}

// §proc_probe
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The window-blocked probe fires.
//
// WHAT IT IS FOR, AND IT IS NOT WHAT THE STALL TURNED OUT TO BE. The
// event-driven retry recovers a window whose reopening is ANNOUNCED; this
// recovers one whose announcement is LOST. No retry can substitute for a
// message that never arrives, and no run so far has produced that case — which
// is exactly why it would be easy to skip now that the symptom is gone. An
// absence that currently produces the donor's behaviour is not agreement with
// the donor.
//
// The donor's mechanism:
//   - a PURE ACK, zero payload, at seq = send_next - 1. Deliberately OUTSIDE
//     the peer's receive window, so the peer must answer it — and it works
//     against a non-mTCP peer because the standard requires an acknowledgement
//     to an unacceptable segment;
//   - gated on peer window <= cwnd AND more than 500 ms since the last
//     acknowledgement WE SENT. Not received;
//   - NO BACKOFF: the probe carries an ACK, so it resets its own clock.
//
// wack_sent is NOT reproduced and that is correct: it is a per-call local on a
// branch that returns immediately, so it cannot affect anything.
//
// ONE DIVERGENCE REMAINS AND IS RECORDED: ours fires from a timer armed on
// refusal, the donor from inside the send path. The donor has two probe sites —
// tcp_out.c:428 tests whether the blocked amount exceeds the peer window, :546
// tests `peer_wnd <= cwnd` — and this matches :546.
void proc_probe(tcp_probe_timeout ev, tcp_ctx ctx) {
    if (ctx.send_wnd > ctx.cwnd)
        return;
    if (now() - ctx.last_ack_sent_ms <= PARITY_PROBE_MS)
        return;

    pkt_gen(ctx, build_hdr(ctx, ctx.send_next - 1, FLAG_ACK, now()),
                    PRIO_ACK, false);
    timer_start(ctx.probe_timer, millisecond(PARITY_PROBE_MS));
    return;
}

// §proc_idle
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The connection has been quiet too long.
//
// THE DONOR TELLS THE APPLICATION WITH AN ERROR, not a clean close
// (timer.c, CheckConnectionTimeout): state goes to CLOSED with close_reason
// TCP_TIMEDOUT, and a stream that still has a socket gets an error event — only
// one with no socket is destroyed silently. NO FIN IS SENT, so the peer learns
// nothing; that is the donor's behaviour and it is why D-27 calls the mechanism
// a workaround for its own close path rather than protocol. The lead still owes
// a decision on whether to keep it.
void proc_idle(tcp_idle_timeout ev, tcp_ctx ctx) {
    ctx.state = ST_CLOSED;
    notify(ctx, ERROR);
    del_ctx(ctx.key);
    return;
}

// §proc_bind
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The application names a local endpoint.
//
// THIS EVENT IS WHAT BRINGS A LISTEN CONTEXT INTO EXISTENCE. It exists after
// bind and is not yet matchable: §net_parser tests for ST_LISTEN, so a SYN for
// a bound-but-not-listening endpoint is dropped. The duplicate-endpoint check
// is here and the donor makes it at listen; ours is here because this is where
// the context is created.
void proc_bind(app_bind ev, tcp_listen_ctx ctx) {
    if (exists(ctx))
        return;                     // already bound
    new_ctx(get_flow_id(ev));           // materialises the context `ctx` names
    ctx.key = tcp_lid(ev.ip, ev.port);
    ctx.local_ip = ev.ip;
    ctx.local_port = ev.port;
    ctx.state = ST_CLOSED;
    return;
}

// §proc_listen
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The endpoint starts answering.
//
// No instructions: it flips a state and sets a bound. Everything that follows
// happens because §net_parser now finds a listen context in ST_LISTEN. The
// analogue of the donor's ListenerHTInsert — bind created the context and
// recorded the endpoint; this is where it becomes matchable.
//
// The application interface needs a handle for the LISTENING endpoint, because
// that is the one that becomes readable when a connection is waiting (CR-2).
void proc_listen(app_listen ev, tcp_listen_ctx ctx) {
    if (!exists(ctx))
        return;                     // listen before bind
    ctx.state = ST_LISTEN;
    ctx.pending_cap = ev.backlog > 0 ? ev.backlog : PROG_MAX_BACKLOG;
    if (ctx.pending_cap > PROG_MAX_BACKLOG)
        ctx.pending_cap = PROG_MAX_BACKLOG;
    ev.opened = ctx.key;
    return;
}

// §proc_accept
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The application takes a pending connection.
//
// THE BACKLOG IS PROTOCOL STATE AND THIS IS WHAT DRAINS IT. §proc_open_done
// adds an entry when a handshake completes; this removes one. Without it
// `pending` would only ever grow and `pending_cap` would start refusing
// connections the application had in fact already taken. Everything else about
// accept — copying the peer address out, allocating a descriptor — is the
// application interface's business; the queue it consumes is the protocol's,
// which is why this is an event rather than a convenience in a shim.
//
// LEVEL-TRIGGERED, and it has to be re-raised HERE. The notification path
// coalesces by kind, so a second completed handshake while the first is still
// queued sets a bit that is already set. Re-raising on every accept that leaves
// the queue non-empty is what keeps the count right — the same shape as the
// receive stream's re-arm inside the read.
void proc_accept(app_accept ev, tcp_listen_ctx ctx) {
    uint32 i;

    if (!exists(ctx) || ctx.state != ST_LISTEN) {
        ev.result = -1;
        return;
    }
    if (ctx.pending_n == 0) {
        ev.result = -1;             // nothing to hand over
        return;
    }

    ev.accepted = ctx.pending[0];
    for (i = 1; i < ctx.pending_n; i = i + 1)
        ctx.pending[i - 1] = ctx.pending[i];
    ctx.pending_n = ctx.pending_n - 1;

    if (ctx.pending_n > 0)
        notify(ctx, READABLE);
    return;
}

// §proc_connect
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The application opens a connection.
//
// It fills in the endpoints the target cannot know — there is no arriving
// packet to take them from — and asks for the SYN. cwnd is NOT set to the
// initial window here: the donor sets cwnd = 1 at connect and turns that into
// mss * TCP_INIT_CWND when the SYN-ACK arrives, so the initial window is a
// property of the handshake completing rather than of asking. `cwnd = 1` means
// "not yet opened", not one byte.
void proc_connect(app_connect ev, tcp_ctx ctx) {
    if (exists(ctx))
        return;                     // already connecting
    new_ctx(get_flow_id(ev));           // materialises the context `ctx` names
    ctx.key = get_flow_id(ev);
    ctx.local_ip = ev.loc_ip;    ctx.remote_ip = ev.rem_ip;
    ctx.loc_port = ev.loc_port;  ctx.rem_port = ev.rem_port;
    ctx.rcv_wnd  = PARITY_INITIAL_WINDOW;
    ctx.snd_base = PARITY_ISN;
    ctx.send_una = PARITY_ISN;
    ctx.send_next = PARITY_ISN;
    ctx.cwnd = 1;
    ctx.ssthresh = PARITY_SSTHRESH_ACTIVE;
    ctx.state = ST_SYN_SENT;
    // No packet created this flow, so the target has no addresses for it.
    ctx_addrs(ctx, ctx.local_ip, ctx.remote_ip);
    // the idle deadline, refreshed; nothing to reap once closed
    if (ctx.state != ST_CLOSED && ctx.state != ST_TIME_WAIT)
        timer_start(ctx.idle_timer, millisecond(PARITY_IDLE_MS));
    ev.opened = ctx.key;                  // the caller needs the handle (CR-2)
    return;
}

// §post_object
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A send with no flow: the object a one-shot server posts before any connection
// exists, so that every accepted connection receives it. It is posted to a
// LISTENER, not to the process — with several listeners there is no "the"
// listener to post to, which is why the op carries the endpoint. §proc_open_done
// is what serves it.
void post_object(app_send ev, tcp_listen_ctx ctx) {
    if (!exists(ctx))
        return;
    ctx.obj = ev.addr;
    ctx.obj_len = ev.mlen;
    return;
}

// §coalesce
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What a merged packet keeps.
//
// TWO CLASSES, which is all TCP needs and is exactly the prototype's three
// merges once they are put on the right axis:
//
//   data      inherit_base = true.  The older sequence and payload origin, the
//                                   newer acknowledgement, window and timestamp.
//   pure ack  inherit_base = false. Both contributions are empty, so the
//                                   emitted acknowledgement carries the LATEST
//                                   cumulative value.
//
// The key is the FLAGS byte, so a data segment never merges with a pure
// acknowledgement, and a SYN, FIN or RST never merges with anything: a control
// packet consumes sequence space and merging it would lose that.
//
// The sequence number describes THIS SEGMENT'S PAYLOAD, so when the merge
// inherits the older payload origin it must inherit this too. Everything else
// in the header describes the receiver's state and must move forward — taking
// the whole newer header took one field too many.
//
// N-B in docs/LANGUAGE-NEEDS.md is that this ought to be a declaration on
// `pkt_bp` rather than a processor, because "which fields the merged packet
// takes" is the content of what goes on the wire and therefore the program's.
// It is written as a processor because the language cannot say it yet.
// A DECLARATION, not a processor -- which is what N-B asked for. Field names
// are TCPBP's.
//
//   standalone    when a blueprint may not merge with anything at all
//   key           two blueprints may merge only if this is equal
//   inherit_base  the merged packet takes the OLDER contribution's payload
//                 origin; everything not named by `keep` moves forward
//   keep          the fields that travel with the payload origin rather than
//                 with the newer header
coalesce tcp_coalesce : TCPBP {
    standalone   (flags & (FLAG_SYN | FLAG_FIN | FLAG_RST)) != 0;
    key          flags;
    inherit_base true;
    keep         seq_no;
}

// §dispatch
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
dispatch tcp_dispatch {
    tcp_syn     -> { proc_passive_open };
    tcp_synack  -> { proc_synack };
    tcp_ack     -> { proc_timestamp, proc_open_done, proc_window, proc_rtt,
                     proc_fast_retransmit, proc_congestion, proc_ack,
                     gen_seg, gen_fin };
    tcp_data    -> { proc_recv, send_ack };
    tcp_fin     -> { proc_fin, send_ack };
    tcp_rst     -> { proc_rst };
    tcp_rto_timeout      -> { proc_rto };
    tcp_timewait_timeout -> { proc_timewait_done };
    tcp_probe_timeout    -> { proc_probe };
    tcp_idle_timeout     -> { proc_idle };
    app_bind    -> { proc_bind };
    app_listen  -> { proc_listen };
    app_accept  -> { proc_accept };
    app_connect -> { proc_connect, gen_syn };
    app_recv    -> { proc_drain };
    app_send    -> { record_data, post_object };
    app_gen     -> { gen_syn, gen_seg, drain_owed_acks, gen_wnd_adv,
                     gen_answer };
    app_close   -> { mark_closed, gen_fin };
}

// THE app_send CHAIN IS ONE CHAIN ON TWO THREADS (CR-4). `record_data` and
// `post_object` are the RECORD half and run inside the application's call;
// `gen_syn`, `gen_seg`, `drain_owed_acks` and `gen_wnd_adv` are the GENERATE
// half and run on the stack. Each guards on `ev.phase_record`, and the
// generated dispatch calls only the half it was asked for, which is knowledge
// the compiler has because it made the placement. `post_object` is selected by
// the CONTEXT TYPE the event resolves to: a send with no flow keys a listener.
//
// `gen_syn` is in this chain as well as in app_connect's because a refused SYN
// — the peer's address is not yet resolved — is re-attempted from the retry
// list, and the retry raises app_send with nothing new to send. One path, not
// two.
//
// §proc_validate AND §gen_rst ARE NOT IN ANY CHAIN, on purpose.
//
// Validation runs before classification -- a segment it refuses raises no
// event, so there is no chain for it to head. gen_rst answers a segment with
// no context, and a chain is dispatched THROUGH a context; there is none to
// dispatch through. Both are named here so that "not in the dispatch" reads
// as a decision rather than an omission. Both live in §net_parser.

// THE CHAINS DO NOT OVERLAP. proc_ack is in the tcp_ack chain and in no other,
// so no processor runs twice for one segment. It headed tcp_data and tcp_fin as
// well while the classification was EXCLUSIVE -- one event per segment, so a
// data segment needed its own way to retire the acknowledgement. Under the
// inclusive classification in §net_parser that is a repeat which buys nothing:
// the only segment raising tcp_data or tcp_fin without also raising tcp_ack is
// one with no ACK flag, and proc_ack's guard refuses that anyway.
//
// THE PER-PROCESSOR `flags & ACK` GUARDS ARE THEREFORE GONE from the tcp_ack
// chain: the event exists only for a segment carrying the flag, so the test is
// true by construction. They are in the emitted C because the chain once
// overlapped; a compiler emitting from this table would not write them.

// gen_fin appears only where it can act. It waits on send_next == write_end,
// and send_next only advances when gen_seg runs, so it follows gen_seg plus the
// close chain that starts it. It is NOT in tcp_data or tcp_fin, which generate
// nothing, and not in app_send, since an application that has closed may not
// send.
// §absence_register
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// MTP is positive-only: a program can say what it does and cannot say what it
// does not. The one rule this effort runs under turns on exactly that — "if one
// stack is missing a mechanism the other has, the two are not running the same
// protocol" — so the absences are declared rather than inferred from silence.
//
// NOT IMPLEMENTED, and each says whether the donor has it, because that is what
// decides whether its absence is a parity difference or a shared property:
//
//   fast_retransmit      CLOSED 2026-08-25, with the duplicate-acknowledgement
//                        change it is paired with (D-30). Demonstrated under
//                        injected loss, not merely compiled: one dropped
//                        segment produced one fast retransmit, sixteen
//                        inflate-only duplicates and ZERO retransmission
//                        timeouts.
//
//   out_of_order_reasm   CLOSED 2026-08-24. §proc_recv marks the arrived range
//                        in rx_wnd and slides; the target's receive stream
//                        stores a segment at its own sequence number instead of
//                        refusing it. Kept visible here for one release so the
//                        register reads as a record rather than a snapshot.
//   window_probe         CLOSED, and it was already closed when this line was
//                        written -- §send_window_probe has existed since D-25
//                        piece 2. VERIFIED against the donor 2026-08-25, not
//                        assumed: tcp_out.c:428-441 probes from the SEND path
//                        when a send is refused and the blocking window is the
//                        PEER's rather than cwnd, more than 500 ms since the
//                        last acknowledgement it sent, as a pure ACK at
//                        snd_nxt - 1 with no backoff.
//
//                        CORRECTED, SAME DAY, after the lead asked whether it
//                        really matched. It did not, and the first version of
//                        this note was written against a description rather
//                        than the code.
//
//                        THE DONOR HAS TWO PROBE SITES, not one. tcp_out.c:428
//                        tests whether the blocked amount exceeds peer_wnd;
//                        :546 tests `peer_wnd <= cwnd`. OURS MATCHES :546 --
//                        the earlier claim of a divergence here was reading
//                        the wrong site.
//
//                        THE REAL DIVERGENCE WAS THE CLOCK, and it was a
//                        defect. ts_lastack_sent is stamped on EVERY segment
//                        carrying the ACK flag (:293), so the donor's "500 ms
//                        since our last acknowledgement" gate is essentially
//                        never satisfied on a busy connection and the probe
//                        fires only once the connection has gone quiet. Ours
//                        stamped it only when a PROBE was emitted, so the gate
//                        measured the interval between probes and we probed
//                        where the donor would not. Fixed: stamped wherever an
//                        acknowledging header is built.
//
//                        ONE DIVERGENCE REMAINS: ours fires from a timer armed
//                        on refusal, the donor from inside the send path.
//
//                        wack_sent is NOT reproduced and that is correct: it is
//                        a per-call local on a branch that returns immediately,
//                        so it cannot affect anything. Re-confirmed here.
//   rst_emission         CLOSED 2026-08-25, both directions.
//                        RECEIVE: §proc_rst, the donor's ProcessRST including
//                        two oddities reproduced on purpose -- a reset on an
//                        ESTABLISHED connection leaves it in CLOSE_WAIT rather
//                        than closed (the donor's destroy is commented out),
//                        and the application is never told WHY
//                        (NotifyConnectionReset is commented out too), so it
//                        learns the socket is readable and reads end-of-stream.
//                        The donor also does not check that the reset's
//                        sequence is in the window -- `TODO: we need reset
//                        validation logic` -- so it is blind-reset-attackable
//                        and so are we. REVISIT before any writeup.
//
//                        EMIT: §gen_rst, and ONLY where the donor emits --
//                        segments belonging to no live connection. The donor
//                        never sends one from an established connection and
//                        mtcp_close always sends a FIN, SO AN ABORTIVE CLOSE
//                        HAS NO REPRESENTATION IN THE DONOR EITHER and adding
//                        one here would be a divergence rather than parity.
//
//                        A full accept backlog DROPS rather than resets: the
//                        donor resets a refused listener and an exhausted flow
//                        pool, and a full accept queue is neither.
//
//                        A4 said this "needs the per-core global ring". THERE
//                        IS NO RING. What the emission path needs from a flow
//                        is a route cache and an IP identifier -- four scalars
//                        per core. Nothing queues: the packet carries no
//                        payload and references no data unit.
//   seq_validation       CLOSED 2026-08-25. §proc_validate, the donor's
//                        ValidateSequence reproduced -- including its test on
//                        seq + payloadlen rather than RFC 793's on first and
//                        last byte separately.
//   paws                 CLOSED 2026-08-25, in the same processor. A segment
//                        from a timestamping peer carrying no timestamp is
//                        dropped, which is the donor's behaviour where the
//                        standard would answer it.
//   idle_reaping         donor HAS it. Still absent. D-27 records that its 30 s
//                        timeout is largely a workaround for its own close
//                        path, so reproducing it reproduces a mechanism whose
//                        necessity is not purely protocol -- a decision the
//                        lead still owes.
//   sack                 NEITHER has it. Adding it would break parity.
//   nagle                NEITHER has it.
//   delayed_ack          NEITHER has it.
//   ecn                  NEITHER has it.
//   keepalive            NEITHER has it.
//   active_open          not needed by the workload; the donor's server path is
//                        passive and that is what is measured.
//
// A compiler should check this register against the body and reject a program
// that implements something it declared absent, or that omits something it did
// not. That check does not exist yet.
